WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.496 --> 00:01.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

00:01.806 --> 00:02.136 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:02.136 --> 00:04.016 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:04.196 --> 00:06.686 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:06.686 --> 00:09.516 align:middle
that Congress has given
us-maximum employment

00:09.516 --> 00:10.426 align:middle
and price stability.

00:11.506 --> 00:12.906 align:middle
Since the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:13.276 --> 00:16.306 align:middle
we have taken forceful actions
to provide relief and stability,

00:16.936 --> 00:19.606 align:middle
to ensure that the recovery
will be as strong as possible,

00:20.246 --> 00:22.316 align:middle
and to limit lasting
damage to the economy.

00:23.656 --> 00:25.616 align:middle
Today my colleagues on the FOMC

00:25.616 --> 00:28.636 align:middle
and I reaffirmed our
strong forward guidance

00:28.636 --> 00:31.876 align:middle
for interest rates and also
provided additional guidance

00:31.876 --> 00:33.436 align:middle
for our asset purchases.

00:34.676 --> 00:36.536 align:middle
Together, these measures
will ensure

00:36.636 --> 00:38.416 align:middle
that monetary policy
will continue

00:38.416 --> 00:40.716 align:middle
to deliver powerful
support to the economy

00:40.796 --> 00:42.436 align:middle
until the recovery is complete.

00:44.326 --> 00:46.896 align:middle
Economic activity has
continued to recover

00:46.896 --> 00:49.176 align:middle
from its depressed
second-quarter level.

00:50.296 --> 00:53.666 align:middle
The substantial reopening of the
economy led to a rapid rebound

00:53.666 --> 00:57.036 align:middle
in activity, and real GDP
rose at an annual rate

00:57.036 --> 00:58.966 align:middle
of 33 percent in
the third quarter.

01:00.146 --> 01:01.146 align:middle
In recent months, however,

01:01.146 --> 01:03.186 align:middle
the pace of improvement
has moderated.

01:04.266 --> 01:07.026 align:middle
Household spending on goods,
especially durable goods,

01:07.406 --> 01:10.316 align:middle
has been strong and has moved
above its pre-pandemic level.

01:11.526 --> 01:14.096 align:middle
In contrast, spending
on services remains low,

01:14.656 --> 01:17.156 align:middle
especially in sectors that
typically require people

01:17.156 --> 01:20.186 align:middle
to gather closely, including
travel and hospitality.

01:21.396 --> 01:24.506 align:middle
The overall rebound in
household spending owes in part

01:24.506 --> 01:27.836 align:middle
to federal stimulus payments and
expanded unemployment benefits,

01:28.366 --> 01:29.836 align:middle
which provided essential support

01:29.836 --> 01:31.506 align:middle
to many families
and individuals.

01:32.696 --> 01:35.226 align:middle
The housing sector has fully
recovered from the downturn,

01:35.456 --> 01:37.996 align:middle
supported in part by low
mortgage interest rates.

01:38.926 --> 01:40.576 align:middle
Business investment
has also picked up.

01:41.386 --> 01:44.716 align:middle
The recovery has progressed more
quickly than generally expected,

01:45.046 --> 01:47.186 align:middle
and forecasts from
FOMC participants

01:47.186 --> 01:49.736 align:middle
for economic growth this
year have been revised

01:49.736 --> 01:52.806 align:middle
up since our September Summary
of Economic Projections.

01:54.316 --> 01:57.976 align:middle
Even so, overall economic
activity remains well below its

01:57.976 --> 01:59.226 align:middle
level before the pandemic,

01:59.646 --> 02:01.836 align:middle
and the path ahead
remains highly uncertain.

02:02.436 --> 02:06.856 align:middle
In the labor market, more than
half of the 22 million jobs

02:06.856 --> 02:09.586 align:middle
that were lost in March and
April have been regained,

02:10.256 --> 02:12.466 align:middle
as many people were
able to return to work.

02:13.816 --> 02:15.716 align:middle
As with overall economic
activity,

02:15.826 --> 02:18.156 align:middle
the pace of improvement in the
labor market has moderated.

02:19.376 --> 02:22.936 align:middle
Job growth slowed to
245,000 in November,

02:23.196 --> 02:25.656 align:middle
and while the unemployment
rate has continued to decline,

02:25.656 --> 02:28.076 align:middle
it remains elevated
at 6.7 percent.

02:29.216 --> 02:32.276 align:middle
Participation in the labor
market remains notably below

02:32.276 --> 02:33.376 align:middle
pre-pandemic levels.

02:34.516 --> 02:36.936 align:middle
Although there has been much
progress in the labor market

02:36.936 --> 02:39.696 align:middle
since the spring, we will not
lose sight of the millions

02:39.696 --> 02:41.416 align:middle
of Americans who
remain out of work.

02:42.736 --> 02:45.866 align:middle
Looking ahead, FOMC participants
project the unemployment rate

02:45.866 --> 02:49.876 align:middle
to continue to decline; the
median projection is 5 percent

02:49.876 --> 02:54.056 align:middle
at the end of next year and
moves below 4 percent by 2023.

02:55.616 --> 02:59.226 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

02:59.446 --> 03:01.726 align:middle
and those least able to shoulder
the burden have been the

03:01.726 --> 03:02.336 align:middle
hardest hit.

03:02.976 --> 03:04.576 align:middle
In particular, the high level

03:04.576 --> 03:06.766 align:middle
of joblessness has
been especially severe

03:06.766 --> 03:08.776 align:middle
for lower-wage workers
in the service sector

03:09.226 --> 03:11.156 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

03:11.636 --> 03:15.126 align:middle
The economic dislocation
has upended many lives

03:15.126 --> 03:17.546 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

03:18.566 --> 03:21.416 align:middle
The pandemic has also
left a significant imprint

03:21.416 --> 03:22.056 align:middle
on inflation.

03:23.326 --> 03:26.926 align:middle
Following large declines in the
spring, consumer prices picked

03:26.926 --> 03:29.786 align:middle
up over the summer but have
leveled out more recently.

03:30.406 --> 03:33.556 align:middle
For those sectors that have
been most adversely affected

03:33.556 --> 03:36.176 align:middle
by the pandemic, prices
remain particularly soft.

03:37.176 --> 03:39.056 align:middle
Overall, on a 12-month basis,

03:39.416 --> 03:42.456 align:middle
inflation remains below our 2
percent longer-run objective.

03:43.696 --> 03:47.476 align:middle
The median inflation projection
from FOMC participants rises

03:47.476 --> 03:50.846 align:middle
from 1.2 percent this year
to 1.8 percent next year

03:51.316 --> 03:53.676 align:middle
and reaches 2 percent in 2023.

03:54.226 --> 03:57.596 align:middle
As we have emphasized
throughout the pandemic,

03:57.596 --> 04:00.256 align:middle
the outlook for the economy
is extraordinarily uncertain

04:00.656 --> 04:03.386 align:middle
and will depend in large part
on the course of the virus.

04:04.556 --> 04:06.816 align:middle
Recent news on vaccines
has been very positive.

04:07.376 --> 04:09.736 align:middle
However, significant challenges
and uncertainties remain

04:09.736 --> 04:12.446 align:middle
with regard to the timing,
production, and distribution

04:12.446 --> 04:14.336 align:middle
of vaccines as well
as their efficacy

04:14.336 --> 04:15.366 align:middle
across different groups.

04:16.196 --> 04:18.606 align:middle
It remains difficult to
assess the timing and scope

04:18.606 --> 04:21.246 align:middle
of the economic implications
of these developments.

04:22.776 --> 04:25.816 align:middle
The ongoing surge in
new COVID-19 cases,

04:25.876 --> 04:27.916 align:middle
both here in the United
States and abroad,

04:28.236 --> 04:31.666 align:middle
is particularly concerning, and
the next few months are likely

04:31.666 --> 04:32.616 align:middle
to be very challenging.

04:33.566 --> 04:34.966 align:middle
All of us have a role to play

04:34.966 --> 04:36.746 align:middle
in our nation's response
to the pandemic.

04:37.496 --> 04:39.646 align:middle
Following the advice of
public health professionals

04:39.646 --> 04:42.446 align:middle
to keep appropriate social
distances and to wear masks

04:42.446 --> 04:45.586 align:middle
in public will help get the
economy back to full strength.

04:46.226 --> 04:49.266 align:middle
A full economic recovery
is unlikely

04:49.266 --> 04:53.216 align:middle
until people are confident that
it is safe to engage-reengage

04:53.216 --> 04:54.566 align:middle
in a broad range of activities.

04:55.096 --> 04:58.316 align:middle
As we previously announced,

04:58.316 --> 05:02.036 align:middle
we are now releasing the entire
package of our SEP materials

05:02.036 --> 05:04.456 align:middle
at the same time as
our FOMC statement.

05:05.276 --> 05:07.846 align:middle
Included in these materials
are two new exhibits

05:08.166 --> 05:10.506 align:middle
that show how the balance
of participants' assessments

05:10.536 --> 05:13.876 align:middle
of uncertainty and risks
have evolved over time.

05:14.736 --> 05:17.906 align:middle
Since the onset of the pandemic,
nearly all participants continue

05:17.906 --> 05:19.626 align:middle
to judge the level
of uncertainty

05:19.626 --> 05:21.586 align:middle
about the economic
outlook as elevated.

05:22.496 --> 05:24.176 align:middle
In terms of risks
to the outlook,

05:24.316 --> 05:26.896 align:middle
fewer participants see the
balance of risks as weighted

05:26.896 --> 05:28.366 align:middle
to the downside than
in September.

05:29.416 --> 05:32.376 align:middle
While a little more than half
of participants now judge risks

05:32.376 --> 05:34.476 align:middle
to be broadly balanced
for economic activity,

05:34.896 --> 05:37.706 align:middle
a similar number continue
to see risks weighted

05:37.706 --> 05:39.226 align:middle
to the downside for inflation.

05:41.256 --> 05:43.776 align:middle
The Fed's response to this
crisis has been guided

05:43.776 --> 05:46.106 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

05:46.106 --> 05:47.936 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

05:48.046 --> 05:50.526 align:middle
along with our responsibilities
to promote the stability

05:50.526 --> 05:51.496 align:middle
of the financial system.

05:52.496 --> 05:54.826 align:middle
As noted in our Statement
on Longer-Run Goals

05:54.826 --> 05:58.196 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy,
we view maximum employment

05:58.196 --> 06:00.086 align:middle
as a broad-based
and inclusive goal.

06:01.336 --> 06:03.326 align:middle
Our ability to achieve
maximum employment

06:03.326 --> 06:04.876 align:middle
in the years ahead
depends importantly

06:04.876 --> 06:08.146 align:middle
on having longer-term inflation
expectations well anchored

06:08.146 --> 06:08.876 align:middle
at 2 percent.

06:09.726 --> 06:11.506 align:middle
As we reiterated in
today's statement,

06:11.506 --> 06:13.806 align:middle
with inflation running
persistently below 2 percent,

06:14.326 --> 06:16.986 align:middle
we will aim to achieve inflation
moderately above 2 percent

06:16.986 --> 06:20.816 align:middle
for some time so that inflation
averages 2 percent over time

06:21.086 --> 06:23.646 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

06:23.646 --> 06:24.746 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

06:26.006 --> 06:28.066 align:middle
We expect to maintain
an accommodative stance

06:28.066 --> 06:29.916 align:middle
of monetary policy
until these employment

06:29.916 --> 06:31.536 align:middle
and inflation outcomes
are achieved.

06:32.916 --> 06:33.976 align:middle
With regard to interest rates,

06:33.976 --> 06:35.826 align:middle
we continue to expect
it will be appropriate

06:35.826 --> 06:39.226 align:middle
to maintain the current 0
to ¼ percent target range

06:39.226 --> 06:40.266 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

06:40.616 --> 06:43.216 align:middle
until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

06:43.216 --> 06:45.186 align:middle
with the Committee's
assessment of maximum employment

06:45.806 --> 06:48.376 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

06:48.376 --> 06:50.536 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

06:51.216 --> 06:54.456 align:middle
In addition, as we noted in
today's policy statement,

06:54.866 --> 06:57.766 align:middle
we will continue to increase our
holdings of Treasury securities

06:57.766 --> 06:59.506 align:middle
by at least $80 billion
per month

06:59.986 --> 07:01.776 align:middle
and of agency mortgage-backed
securities

07:01.776 --> 07:03.986 align:middle
by at least $40 billion
per month

07:03.986 --> 07:07.286 align:middle
until substantial further
progress has been made toward

07:07.286 --> 07:09.866 align:middle
our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

07:10.816 --> 07:11.876 align:middle
We believe the increase

07:12.166 --> 07:15.186 align:middle
in our balance sheet this year
has materially eased financial

07:15.186 --> 07:17.956 align:middle
conditions and is
providing substantial support

07:17.956 --> 07:18.616 align:middle
to the economy.

07:20.076 --> 07:21.696 align:middle
Combined with our
forward guidance

07:21.696 --> 07:22.796 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,

07:22.796 --> 07:25.266 align:middle
our enhanced balance
sheet guidance will ensure

07:25.266 --> 07:28.046 align:middle
that the stance of monetary
policy remains highly

07:28.046 --> 07:30.816 align:middle
accommodative as the
recovery progresses.

07:31.596 --> 07:33.716 align:middle
Our guidance is outcome
based and is tied

07:33.716 --> 07:36.356 align:middle
to progress toward reaching our
employment and inflation goals.

07:36.836 --> 07:39.046 align:middle
Thus, if progress toward
our goals were to slow,

07:39.336 --> 07:40.866 align:middle
the guidance would
convey our intention

07:40.866 --> 07:44.636 align:middle
to increase policy accommodation
through a lower expected path

07:44.636 --> 07:45.576 align:middle
of the federal funds rate

07:45.576 --> 07:48.296 align:middle
and a higher expected
path of the balance sheet.

07:48.946 --> 07:50.226 align:middle
Overall, our interest rate

07:50.226 --> 07:52.776 align:middle
and balance sheet tools are
providing powerful support

07:52.776 --> 07:54.976 align:middle
to the economy and
will continue to do so.

07:56.356 --> 07:58.636 align:middle
The Federal Reserve has
also been taking broad

07:58.636 --> 08:01.016 align:middle
and forceful actions to more
directly support the flow

08:01.016 --> 08:03.956 align:middle
of credit in the economy for
households, for businesses large

08:03.956 --> 08:06.176 align:middle
and small, and for state
and local governments.

08:07.036 --> 08:09.796 align:middle
Preserving the flow of credit is
essential for mitigating damage

08:09.796 --> 08:12.096 align:middle
to the economy and
promoting a robust recovery.

08:13.496 --> 08:16.686 align:middle
Many of our programs rely
on emergency lending powers

08:16.686 --> 08:18.646 align:middle
that require the support
of the Treasury Department

08:18.646 --> 08:21.736 align:middle
and are available only in
very unusual circumstances,

08:22.086 --> 08:23.896 align:middle
such as those we find
ourselves in today.

08:25.186 --> 08:28.126 align:middle
These programs serve as a
backstop to key credit markets

08:28.126 --> 08:29.876 align:middle
and have helped to
restore the flow of credit

08:29.876 --> 08:31.786 align:middle
from private lenders
through normal channels.

08:31.866 --> 08:33.996 align:middle
We have deployed
these lending powers

08:33.996 --> 08:36.966 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled in large part

08:36.966 --> 08:39.646 align:middle
by financial backing and support
from Congress and the Treasury.

08:40.786 --> 08:43.136 align:middle
Although funds from the CARES
Act will not be available

08:43.136 --> 08:46.086 align:middle
to support new loans or new
purchases after-of assets

08:46.086 --> 08:50.136 align:middle
after December 31, the
Treasury could authorize support

08:50.136 --> 08:51.876 align:middle
for emerging lending
facilities, if needed,

08:51.916 --> 08:53.426 align:middle
through the Exchange
Stabilization Fund.

08:54.026 --> 08:56.516 align:middle
When the time comes, after
the crisis has passed,

08:56.936 --> 08:59.256 align:middle
we will put these emergency
tools back in the box.

08:59.766 --> 09:02.146 align:middle
As I have emphasized before,

09:02.326 --> 09:04.696 align:middle
these are lending powers,
not spending powers.

09:04.996 --> 09:07.556 align:middle
The Fed cannot grant money
to particular beneficiaries.

09:08.496 --> 09:10.546 align:middle
We can only create
programs or facilities

09:10.546 --> 09:13.966 align:middle
with broad-based eligibility to
make loans to solvent entities

09:14.206 --> 09:16.446 align:middle
with the expectation that
the loans will be repaid.

09:17.416 --> 09:19.886 align:middle
Many borrowers are
benefiting from these programs,

09:20.086 --> 09:21.856 align:middle
as is the overall economy.

09:22.836 --> 09:25.346 align:middle
But for many others, getting
a loan that may be difficult

09:25.346 --> 09:26.966 align:middle
to repay may not be the answer.

09:27.656 --> 09:30.676 align:middle
In these cases, direct
fiscal support may be needed.

09:31.716 --> 09:33.976 align:middle
Elected officials have
the power to tax and spend

09:33.976 --> 09:36.456 align:middle
and to make decisions about
where we, as a society,

09:36.456 --> 09:38.276 align:middle
should direct our
collective resources.

09:39.366 --> 09:40.656 align:middle
The fiscal policy actions

09:40.656 --> 09:44.086 align:middle
that have been taken thus far
have made a critical difference

09:44.086 --> 09:46.826 align:middle
to families, businesses, and
communities across the country.

09:47.916 --> 09:51.056 align:middle
Even so, the current economic
downturn is the most severe

09:51.056 --> 09:51.936 align:middle
of our lifetimes.

09:52.336 --> 09:54.516 align:middle
It will take a while to
get back to the levels

09:54.516 --> 09:57.196 align:middle
of economic activity and
employment that prevailed

09:57.196 --> 09:58.236 align:middle
at the beginning of this year,

09:58.536 --> 10:01.106 align:middle
and it may take continued
support from both monetary

10:01.106 --> 10:03.416 align:middle
and fiscal policy
to achieve that.

10:04.576 --> 10:07.786 align:middle
To conclude, we understand that
our actions affect communities,

10:07.956 --> 10:10.086 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

10:10.836 --> 10:13.196 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

10:13.886 --> 10:16.016 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

10:16.016 --> 10:18.946 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

10:18.946 --> 10:22.376 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

10:23.396 --> 10:23.766 align:middle
Thank you.

10:23.946 --> 10:25.146 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

10:25.246 --> 10:25.666 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:28.216 --> 10:28.986 align:middle
Thank you.

10:28.986 --> 10:29.506 align:middle
Rich Miller.

10:29.506 --> 10:29.936 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

10:30.666 --> 10:32.726 align:middle
Thank you very much,
Mr. Chairman.

10:32.726 --> 10:33.866 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

10:34.696 --> 10:39.276 align:middle
I wonder if I could
start with the, the SEP.

10:39.276 --> 10:41.816 align:middle
We have-unemployment
now is at 6.7,

10:42.286 --> 10:44.936 align:middle
the SEP median sees,
sees it falling to 5.

10:45.116 --> 10:47.816 align:middle
Headline inflation
is one time 1.2,

10:47.876 --> 10:50.096 align:middle
the SEP median sees
it rising to 1.8.

10:50.096 --> 10:51.606 align:middle
And core inflation is 1.4,

10:51.606 --> 10:55.076 align:middle
and the SEP sees it
rising to 1.8 likewise.

10:55.076 --> 10:56.146 align:middle
Would that constitute

10:56.146 --> 10:58.766 align:middle
"substantial further progress"
toward the Committee's

10:58.766 --> 11:01.116 align:middle
maximum-employment and
price-stability goals?

11:02.036 --> 11:02.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:02.526 --> 11:04.276 align:middle
Yes. Well, so we're
not-we're not going

11:04.276 --> 11:06.746 align:middle
to be identifying
specific-associating

11:06.746 --> 11:10.676 align:middle
that test with, with specific
numbers at this point.

11:11.066 --> 11:13.046 align:middle
So, really, the question is,

11:13.046 --> 11:16.576 align:middle
what do we mean by,
by that language?

11:17.816 --> 11:21.076 align:middle
And, really, the
overarching message, Rich,

11:21.136 --> 11:23.036 align:middle
is that our guidance
for both interest rates

11:23.036 --> 11:26.746 align:middle
and asset purchases will keep
monetary policy accommodative

11:26.746 --> 11:28.516 align:middle
until our maximum-employment

11:28.516 --> 11:30.486 align:middle
and price-stability goals
are achieved, and that's,

11:30.486 --> 11:31.816 align:middle
that's a powerful message.

11:32.486 --> 11:36.146 align:middle
So "substantial further
progress" means what it says.

11:36.466 --> 11:37.766 align:middle
It means we'll be
looking for employment

11:37.766 --> 11:41.126 align:middle
to be substantially closer to
assessments of its maximum level

11:41.266 --> 11:43.656 align:middle
and inflation to be
substantially closer

11:43.656 --> 11:46.646 align:middle
to our 2 percent longer-run
goal before we start making

11:46.646 --> 11:47.936 align:middle
adjustments to our purchases.

11:48.836 --> 11:52.686 align:middle
I would also point out that by
increasing our asset holdings,

11:52.826 --> 11:55.556 align:middle
we see ourselves as adding
policy accommodation.

11:56.026 --> 11:58.566 align:middle
There will come a time when
the economy does not require

11:58.566 --> 12:00.986 align:middle
increasing amounts of
policy accommodation.

12:00.986 --> 12:04.516 align:middle
And when that time comes-and
that will be uncertain and,

12:04.646 --> 12:06.526 align:middle
in any case, is,
is some ways off.

12:07.226 --> 12:12.136 align:middle
So I can't give you an
exact set of numbers.

12:12.136 --> 12:14.866 align:middle
We will-of course, as
we approach that point,

12:14.866 --> 12:16.096 align:middle
we'll be evaluating that.

12:16.096 --> 12:19.216 align:middle
And when we see ourselves on
a path to achieve that goal,

12:19.856 --> 12:23.766 align:middle
then we will-we will say so
undoubtedly well in advance

12:23.846 --> 12:26.686 align:middle
of any time when we would
actually consider gradually

12:26.686 --> 12:28.566 align:middle
tapering the pace of purchases.

12:30.336 --> 12:30.776 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

12:30.776 --> 12:35.106 align:middle
Very quickly on, on that.

12:35.106 --> 12:37.626 align:middle
You, you mentioned the next
couple months are going

12:37.626 --> 12:39.226 align:middle
to be very challenging.

12:39.326 --> 12:43.936 align:middle
And regarding asset purchases,
why not increase the duration

12:43.936 --> 12:46.756 align:middle
of the asset purchases at
this time, given what you see

12:46.756 --> 12:49.216 align:middle
as a very challenging
period ahead?

12:49.216 --> 12:49.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:49.516 --> 12:53.506 align:middle
So, you know, again, I would
start with what we actually did

12:53.506 --> 12:56.136 align:middle
at the meeting, which,
which was, we provided this,

12:56.186 --> 12:59.126 align:middle
this guidance about the
path of asset purchases.

12:59.126 --> 13:01.026 align:middle
And I just went through what,

13:01.026 --> 13:05.156 align:middle
what they-what they-the
guidance that we put forward.

13:05.746 --> 13:07.976 align:middle
And, you know, so I guess

13:07.976 --> 13:11.476 align:middle
since September we've now
adopted a flexible average

13:11.476 --> 13:13.836 align:middle
inflation-targeting framework.

13:14.686 --> 13:18.536 align:middle
We have provided rate
guidance that is tightly linked

13:18.816 --> 13:22.016 align:middle
to the goals as expressed
in that new framework.

13:22.016 --> 13:25.286 align:middle
And now we've done the same
for, for asset purchases.

13:26.046 --> 13:28.776 align:middle
So, you know, we've,
we've been sort of-as,

13:28.776 --> 13:32.276 align:middle
as the future has become clearer

13:32.586 --> 13:35.006 align:middle
and as we've absorbed
new developments

13:35.006 --> 13:39.076 align:middle
in medical-in the medical sphere
and also in, in the economy,

13:39.546 --> 13:41.266 align:middle
we have-began to be
able to see further.

13:41.266 --> 13:42.386 align:middle
So we've, we've started
to be able

13:42.386 --> 13:44.956 align:middle
to provide further
important guidance.

13:44.956 --> 13:47.456 align:middle
And we think that the-that
the asset purchase guidance,

13:47.456 --> 13:48.706 align:middle
guidance is very important.

13:49.036 --> 13:51.126 align:middle
We think that the,
the prior language

13:51.126 --> 13:54.256 align:middle
of "in coming months"
was obviously temporary.

13:54.696 --> 13:57.496 align:middle
This links that guidance,
those purchases,

13:57.496 --> 14:01.686 align:middle
to actual "substantial
further progress" toward our,

14:02.056 --> 14:03.006 align:middle
our mandated goals.

14:03.006 --> 14:04.106 align:middle
We think that's important.

14:04.686 --> 14:08.056 align:middle
And we think that that is
important to have done.

14:08.056 --> 14:11.426 align:middle
I, I would just add, though,
that we also continue to think

14:11.426 --> 14:13.556 align:middle
that our current policy
stance is appropriate.

14:14.226 --> 14:17.376 align:middle
We think it's providing a great
deal of support for the economy.

14:17.746 --> 14:19.996 align:middle
Financial conditions are
highly accommodative.

14:20.346 --> 14:22.826 align:middle
And, you know, we
monitor a range

14:22.826 --> 14:24.346 align:middle
of financial condition indexes.

14:24.346 --> 14:25.536 align:middle
There are many of
them, and they'll,

14:25.826 --> 14:27.146 align:middle
they'll all pretty
much tell you that.

14:27.666 --> 14:30.286 align:middle
You can-you can also look at
the interest sensitive parts

14:30.286 --> 14:33.566 align:middle
of the economy-for example,
housing, durable sales,

14:33.566 --> 14:35.716 align:middle
vehicle sales-those
parts of the economy are,

14:36.056 --> 14:37.456 align:middle
are performing very, very well.

14:37.656 --> 14:38.586 align:middle
The parts of the economy

14:38.586 --> 14:40.866 align:middle
that are weak are the
service-sector businesses

14:40.866 --> 14:42.166 align:middle
that involve close contact.

14:42.926 --> 14:45.256 align:middle
Those are not being held
back by financial conditions,

14:45.746 --> 14:47.246 align:middle
but rather by the
spread of the virus.

14:47.886 --> 14:49.636 align:middle
And I'll close by,
by just saying,

14:50.006 --> 14:52.676 align:middle
we do have the flexibility
to provide more accommodation

14:52.676 --> 14:53.916 align:middle
through the channel
you mentioned

14:53.916 --> 14:54.946 align:middle
and through other channels.

14:55.546 --> 14:58.916 align:middle
And we recognize that
circumstances could shift

14:58.916 --> 15:01.086 align:middle
in a way that warrants
our, our doing that,

15:01.086 --> 15:02.646 align:middle
including by adjusting
purchases.

15:03.226 --> 15:06.026 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

15:06.026 --> 15:08.536 align:middle
to support the U.S. economy
to achieve our goals.

15:09.106 --> 15:11.416 align:middle
We will continue to use our
tools to support the economy

15:11.416 --> 15:15.376 align:middle
for as long as it takes until
the job is well and truly done.

15:15.956 --> 15:16.806 align:middle
No one should doubt that.

15:16.806 --> 15:17.386 align:middle
RICH MILLER.

15:18.436 --> 15:18.756 align:middle
Thank you.

15:19.026 --> 15:20.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:20.466 --> 15:20.976 align:middle
Thank you.

15:20.976 --> 15:21.936 align:middle
Nick Timiraos.

15:22.046 --> 15:22.766 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:24.566 --> 15:25.286 align:middle
Thank you.

15:25.286 --> 15:27.916 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, the
Wall Street Journal.

15:27.916 --> 15:31.056 align:middle
Chair Powell, a two-part
question.

15:31.056 --> 15:33.646 align:middle
What, if anything,
would prompt the Fed

15:33.646 --> 15:37.086 align:middle
to shift Treasury purchases
toward the longer end

15:37.086 --> 15:40.596 align:middle
of the curve as you did
with your prior QE programs?

15:41.066 --> 15:43.056 align:middle
And does your guidance today

15:43.056 --> 15:47.316 align:middle
on asset purchases foreclose
the possibility that you could,

15:47.316 --> 15:51.006 align:middle
at some point, lengthen
maturities while simultaneously

15:51.006 --> 15:54.046 align:middle
tapering the monthly
purchase amounts?

15:54.296 --> 15:55.466 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:56.136 --> 15:59.096 align:middle
Well, Nick, I-on, on the
first part of the question, I,

15:59.176 --> 16:02.366 align:middle
I don't-I wouldn't
want to sort of talk

16:02.366 --> 16:03.916 align:middle
about hypothetical situations.

16:03.966 --> 16:07.466 align:middle
You know, we, we look at our
overall stance of policy,

16:07.466 --> 16:09.026 align:middle
we look at overall
financial conditions,

16:09.026 --> 16:11.506 align:middle
we look at what's going on in
the economy-different parts

16:11.506 --> 16:13.076 align:middle
of the economy, and
we ask ourselves,

16:13.526 --> 16:14.836 align:middle
should we change
our policy stance?

16:14.836 --> 16:15.936 align:middle
We do that at every meeting.

16:16.416 --> 16:19.376 align:middle
And we look at where financial
conditions are now, and, and we,

16:19.376 --> 16:22.936 align:middle
we feel that they are
appropriate for now.

16:23.366 --> 16:26.386 align:middle
Anytime we feel like
the economy could,

16:26.386 --> 16:29.936 align:middle
could use stronger
accommodation,

16:30.116 --> 16:31.716 align:middle
we would be prepared
to provide it.

16:32.006 --> 16:33.956 align:middle
We-but right now, we're
providing a great deal,

16:33.956 --> 16:35.706 align:middle
and we think-we happen to
think it's the right amount.

16:36.316 --> 16:37.656 align:middle
And, you know, you,

16:37.976 --> 16:40.386 align:middle
you mentioned-I think
you're referring to the idea

16:40.386 --> 16:45.376 align:middle
of maintaining the duration
but, but reducing the quantity,

16:45.836 --> 16:47.996 align:middle
which is sort of what
the Bank of Canada did.

16:48.446 --> 16:50.926 align:middle
And that, that is something
that we-you know, that we talked

16:50.926 --> 16:52.936 align:middle
about in the last meeting and
was addressed in the minutes.

16:52.936 --> 16:54.616 align:middle
And I would say, the
views on that were mixed:

16:54.616 --> 16:55.916 align:middle
Some thought it was
an interesting idea,

16:55.916 --> 16:56.846 align:middle
others not so much.

16:57.446 --> 16:59.406 align:middle
I, I wouldn't say that
that's something that's,

16:59.516 --> 17:01.846 align:middle
that's high on our list
of, of possibilities.

17:01.916 --> 17:02.456 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:03.766 --> 17:04.056 align:middle
Thank you.

17:04.056 --> 17:04.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:06.336 --> 17:06.896 align:middle
Thank you.

17:06.896 --> 17:10.646 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

17:10.646 --> 17:12.036 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

17:12.036 --> 17:15.856 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
the 13(3) facilities.

17:15.946 --> 17:16.886 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've said

17:16.886 --> 17:19.076 align:middle
that you accept Treasury
Secretary Mnuchin's

17:19.076 --> 17:21.856 align:middle
interpretation of the
statute of the CARES Act on,

17:21.856 --> 17:23.816 align:middle
on what should happen
with those programs.

17:23.816 --> 17:26.456 align:middle
So, first of all,
I'm curious whether

17:26.456 --> 17:30.306 align:middle
under a new Treasury Secretary
you will accept whatever legal

17:30.306 --> 17:32.706 align:middle
interpretation they put
forward for those programs.

17:32.706 --> 17:36.936 align:middle
And then, also, given that there
isn't a statutory requirement

17:36.936 --> 17:39.246 align:middle
for you to have financial
backing from Treasury

17:39.246 --> 17:41.996 align:middle
for 13(3) facilities,
do you have any plans

17:42.166 --> 17:45.326 align:middle
for any future facilities that
don't require Treasury backing?

17:45.586 --> 17:47.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:47.796 --> 17:49.746 align:middle
So on your-on your
first question,

17:49.746 --> 17:52.066 align:middle
we really have not
thought about that.

17:52.066 --> 17:54.386 align:middle
We're, we're very focused
on-we have a lot to do now.

17:54.386 --> 17:58.676 align:middle
We, we have not focused on,
on that question, and I,

17:58.746 --> 18:00.566 align:middle
I really have nothing
to add on that.

18:00.926 --> 18:03.776 align:middle
Your second question is-sorry.

18:03.776 --> 18:07.546 align:middle
Oh, if there were no-yes.

18:07.546 --> 18:10.186 align:middle
No, we, we would have
the ability-certainly,

18:10.186 --> 18:13.056 align:middle
we would have the ability
to do facilities under 13(3)

18:13.736 --> 18:15.276 align:middle
in some cases with no backing.

18:15.276 --> 18:18.836 align:middle
But we can't do any 13(3)
facilities without the approval

18:18.836 --> 18:20.266 align:middle
of the Treasury Secretary,
right?

18:20.396 --> 18:23.426 align:middle
But we did some facilities-I
think one

18:23.426 --> 18:25.516 align:middle
of our facilities this
time didn't have any

18:25.886 --> 18:26.646 align:middle
Treasury backing.

18:27.176 --> 18:31.266 align:middle
And I think some in the-in
the round of-during the Global

18:31.266 --> 18:32.806 align:middle
Financial Crisis
also didn't have any.

18:33.186 --> 18:34.926 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

18:35.106 --> 18:36.746 align:middle
So do you have any plans-like,

18:36.746 --> 18:38.636 align:middle
would you consider
doing more facilities

18:38.636 --> 18:40.596 align:middle
if that became necessary
without Treasury backing?

18:40.596 --> 18:40.966 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

18:40.966 --> 18:42.686 align:middle
I would say that
we are-you know,

18:42.686 --> 18:45.036 align:middle
we're-we have the
authorities we have.

18:45.186 --> 18:50.416 align:middle
We will use them if, if
they're needed and if-and

18:50.416 --> 18:52.186 align:middle
if the law permits us to do so.

18:52.756 --> 18:54.076 align:middle
We would-we would
always do that.

18:54.076 --> 18:58.006 align:middle
We, we do not have any plans
for, for the future about this.

18:58.006 --> 19:00.696 align:middle
We-we're very focused on,
on getting through year-end.

19:01.066 --> 19:03.686 align:middle
We've been very focused
on, on the issues

19:03.686 --> 19:04.646 align:middle
that are right in front of us.

19:04.646 --> 19:07.946 align:middle
And, honestly, we're not-we're
not planning on anything

19:07.946 --> 19:11.156 align:middle
or having any discussions about
what we might do down the road.

19:11.156 --> 19:11.796 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

19:13.686 --> 19:13.956 align:middle
Thank you.

19:14.056 --> 19:15.056 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:15.266 --> 19:15.916 align:middle
Thank you.

19:15.916 --> 19:16.686 align:middle
Steve Liesman.

19:16.836 --> 19:18.576 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

19:18.706 --> 19:21.366 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman,
and happy New Year.

19:21.366 --> 19:25.156 align:middle
I know how much you enjoy
talking about fiscal stimulus,

19:25.156 --> 19:27.346 align:middle
so let me ask you directly
about fiscal stimulus.

19:27.696 --> 19:32.006 align:middle
There's talk right now of a $900
billion fiscal stimulus program

19:32.006 --> 19:33.536 align:middle
in Congress.

19:33.536 --> 19:34.736 align:middle
Is, is that sufficient?

19:34.736 --> 19:36.786 align:middle
Is that what you're looking for?

19:36.786 --> 19:38.056 align:middle
Do you think that'll
be sufficient

19:38.056 --> 19:39.036 align:middle
for helping the economy?

19:39.036 --> 19:41.266 align:middle
Finally, you talked
about the idea

19:41.266 --> 19:44.266 align:middle
of how concerning
the recent surge is.

19:44.266 --> 19:47.436 align:middle
I wonder if you can put
some detail on that.

19:47.436 --> 19:49.096 align:middle
How much concern do
you have in terms of,

19:49.406 --> 19:50.686 align:middle
where could employment go?

19:50.686 --> 19:52.956 align:middle
What could happen to
GDP in the first quarter

19:52.956 --> 19:54.076 align:middle
as a result of this surge?

19:54.076 --> 19:54.466 align:middle
Thank you.

19:54.526 --> 19:54.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:55.636 --> 19:58.076 align:middle
Okay. So on, on fiscal policy,

19:58.466 --> 20:01.356 align:middle
I would just say a
couple of things.

20:01.356 --> 20:06.096 align:middle
The case for fiscal policy right
now is, is very, very strong.

20:06.306 --> 20:10.046 align:middle
And I think that is widely
understood, I would say, now.

20:10.636 --> 20:13.106 align:middle
The details of it are
entirely up to Congress.

20:13.576 --> 20:16.616 align:middle
But with the expiration of
unemployment benefits-some

20:16.616 --> 20:18.596 align:middle
of the unemployment
benefits, the expiration

20:18.696 --> 20:23.166 align:middle
of eviction moratoriums with the
virus spreading the way it is,

20:23.656 --> 20:25.306 align:middle
there's a need for
households and businesses

20:25.306 --> 20:27.766 align:middle
to have, have fiscal support.

20:27.766 --> 20:30.776 align:middle
And I do think that-again, I
think that is widely understood.

20:31.256 --> 20:36.846 align:middle
So I think it would be-I, I
certainly would welcome the work

20:36.846 --> 20:38.036 align:middle
that Congress is
doing right now.

20:38.036 --> 20:39.466 align:middle
It's not up to us
to judge that work.

20:39.466 --> 20:40.786 align:middle
It's really, really theirs.

20:41.346 --> 20:44.146 align:middle
And I, I don't have a view
for you on, on the size of it.

20:44.146 --> 20:46.666 align:middle
It's-you know, it's
obviously a substantial bill.

20:47.346 --> 20:52.046 align:middle
On the surge, you know, so it's
a really interesting question.

20:53.386 --> 20:59.046 align:middle
We have, and others too, have
consistently expected there

20:59.046 --> 21:05.286 align:middle
to be more economic-that,
that cases would hold-growth

21:05.286 --> 21:07.616 align:middle
in cases would hold
back the economy more

21:07.616 --> 21:08.646 align:middle
than it actually has.

21:09.216 --> 21:12.266 align:middle
So we've overestimated the,
the effects on the economy of,

21:12.886 --> 21:14.126 align:middle
of these spikes we've
been having.

21:14.266 --> 21:16.126 align:middle
Now, this spike is
so much larger

21:16.716 --> 21:19.646 align:middle
that I think-and I think
forecasters generally do think

21:19.646 --> 21:22.886 align:middle
that there-that this
will have an effect

21:22.886 --> 21:25.286 align:middle
on suppressing activity,
particularly activity

21:25.286 --> 21:27.646 align:middle
that involves people
getting together in bars

21:27.646 --> 21:29.986 align:middle
and restaurants, on airplanes
and hotels and things like that.

21:30.426 --> 21:31.506 align:middle
And you're starting to see that.

21:31.506 --> 21:32.866 align:middle
In the-in the high-frequency
data,

21:33.136 --> 21:34.826 align:middle
you're now beginning
to see that show up.

21:34.826 --> 21:37.756 align:middle
And I think if this is-the
case numbers are so high

21:38.146 --> 21:40.436 align:middle
and so widespread across
the country that that,

21:40.806 --> 21:42.116 align:middle
that seems like it must happen.

21:42.116 --> 21:43.196 align:middle
Now, how big will it be?

21:43.196 --> 21:44.536 align:middle
We don't-we don't really know.

21:44.866 --> 21:47.256 align:middle
You know, there are-there
are a lot of estimates.

21:47.256 --> 21:50.546 align:middle
The general expectation is,
you're seeing some slowing now.

21:51.066 --> 21:53.546 align:middle
And you'll see the first
quarter could well be-what,

21:53.546 --> 21:55.076 align:middle
what we said is that
coming months are going

21:55.076 --> 21:55.756 align:middle
to be challenging.

21:56.316 --> 21:57.256 align:middle
The first quarter will,

21:57.436 --> 21:59.996 align:middle
will certainly show
significant effects from this.

22:00.656 --> 22:03.496 align:middle
At the same time, people
are getting vaccinated now.

22:03.656 --> 22:04.706 align:middle
They're getting vaccinated.

22:04.706 --> 22:07.936 align:middle
And by the end of the first
quarter into the second quarter,

22:08.366 --> 22:10.716 align:middle
you're going to be
seeing significant numbers

22:10.716 --> 22:11.846 align:middle
of people vaccinated.

22:11.846 --> 22:14.216 align:middle
And so then what will
be-how will that play

22:14.216 --> 22:15.256 align:middle
into economic activity?

22:15.256 --> 22:16.946 align:middle
Again, we don't have any
experience with this.

22:17.286 --> 22:20.936 align:middle
You have to think that sometime
in the middle of next year,

22:20.936 --> 22:24.686 align:middle
you will-you will see people
feeling comfortable going out

22:24.966 --> 22:27.646 align:middle
and engaging in a broader
range of activities.

22:27.646 --> 22:29.916 align:middle
And some people will be
probably quick to do that.

22:29.916 --> 22:34.346 align:middle
Some are doing it now without
a vaccine-right?-in many parts

22:34.346 --> 22:34.786 align:middle
of the country.

22:34.786 --> 22:36.216 align:middle
So, nonetheless,

22:36.936 --> 22:40.446 align:middle
my expectation-and I think many
people have the expectation

22:40.446 --> 22:43.896 align:middle
that the second half of next
year should-the economy should

22:43.896 --> 22:45.076 align:middle
be performing strongly.

22:45.076 --> 22:49.216 align:middle
We should be-you know, we should
be getting people back to work.

22:49.376 --> 22:51.276 align:middle
Businesses should be reopening
and that kind of thing.

22:51.516 --> 22:53.816 align:middle
The issue is more the next
four or five months-getting

22:53.816 --> 22:55.306 align:middle
through the next four,
five, six months.

22:55.926 --> 22:56.756 align:middle
That is key.

22:56.756 --> 22:58.376 align:middle
And, and, you know,
clearly there's going

22:58.376 --> 22:59.516 align:middle
to be need for help there.

22:59.516 --> 23:02.706 align:middle
And, and, you know,
my sense and hope is

23:02.706 --> 23:03.596 align:middle
that we'll be getting that.

23:03.596 --> 23:04.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:07.246 --> 23:07.786 align:middle
Thank you.

23:08.236 --> 23:08.766 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek.

23:08.766 --> 23:09.716 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

23:12.906 --> 23:14.006 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

23:14.006 --> 23:15.806 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

23:15.806 --> 23:18.716 align:middle
I was wondering if you
could talk a little bit

23:18.716 --> 23:20.736 align:middle
about house prices.

23:20.736 --> 23:23.686 align:middle
They were mentioned in the most
recent minutes as, you know,

23:23.686 --> 23:26.996 align:middle
potentially a valuation concern,
especially as it related

23:26.996 --> 23:28.646 align:middle
to your mortgage-backed
security purchases.

23:28.646 --> 23:30.256 align:middle
And you mentioned earlier today

23:30.256 --> 23:33.326 align:middle
that the housing market looks
more or less fully healed.

23:33.326 --> 23:35.036 align:middle
And so I guess I
wonder, are you worried

23:35.036 --> 23:36.636 align:middle
about valuation pressures there?

23:36.636 --> 23:39.006 align:middle
And, if so, what can the
Fed do to contain those?

23:39.006 --> 23:40.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:40.726 --> 23:44.776 align:middle
You know, so we monitor
basically just

23:44.776 --> 23:46.976 align:middle
about every asset
price in the economy,

23:46.976 --> 23:49.076 align:middle
and housing prices are something
that we've been monitoring.

23:49.556 --> 23:51.786 align:middle
And you see them moving up.

23:51.786 --> 23:53.776 align:middle
You see very high demand.

23:53.846 --> 23:55.216 align:middle
This, this is the housing market

23:55.216 --> 23:59.456 align:middle
that people have been
expecting since, you know, 2010.

23:59.456 --> 24:01.546 align:middle
And, and then, you
know, not many,

24:01.606 --> 24:03.776 align:middle
when the-when the
pandemic hit, thought, "Oh,

24:03.776 --> 24:05.186 align:middle
this is what-this
is what will produce

24:05.186 --> 24:06.456 align:middle
that housing market,"
but it has.

24:07.246 --> 24:09.656 align:middle
Now, I would say, from a
financial stability standpoint,

24:09.656 --> 24:14.176 align:middle
housing prices are not-are not
of a level of concern right now.

24:14.716 --> 24:16.516 align:middle
That's just reflective
of a lot of demand.

24:17.016 --> 24:19.686 align:middle
And, you know, builders are
going to bring forth supply.

24:19.686 --> 24:21.106 align:middle
There's also a sense
that this-some

24:21.106 --> 24:22.586 align:middle
of this may be pent-up demand

24:22.586 --> 24:24.366 align:middle
from when the economy was
closed, which would imply

24:24.366 --> 24:27.396 align:middle
that demand-once that demand
is met, that the real level

24:27.396 --> 24:29.426 align:middle
of demand will be
more manageable.

24:29.426 --> 24:32.656 align:middle
So it's a-it's a
healthy economy now.

24:32.656 --> 24:35.166 align:middle
We, we met recently with
a bunch of homebuilders,

24:35.166 --> 24:36.746 align:middle
and many of them
in the business 30,

24:36.746 --> 24:38.716 align:middle
40 years said they've
never seen the likes of it.

24:39.026 --> 24:41.976 align:middle
But, no, housing prices
themselves are not-are not a

24:42.206 --> 24:44.796 align:middle
financial stability
concern at the moment.

24:45.466 --> 24:46.736 align:middle
We will watch that carefully.

24:46.736 --> 24:49.046 align:middle
But in the near term, I wouldn't
think that that's an issue

24:49.166 --> 24:50.256 align:middle
that we'd be concerned about.

24:50.416 --> 24:50.966 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

24:51.026 --> 24:53.136 align:middle
Thanks. MICHELLE SMITH.

24:53.516 --> 24:54.326 align:middle
Thank you.

24:54.376 --> 24:57.556 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

24:57.556 --> 24:58.136 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

24:58.136 --> 25:00.036 align:middle
Thank you for the
question, Mr. Chairman.

25:00.036 --> 25:02.496 align:middle
So with the actions that
you've currently taken,

25:02.496 --> 25:05.306 align:middle
how do you-how do you
further fight a slowdown

25:05.306 --> 25:05.906 align:middle
that you're seeing?

25:05.906 --> 25:08.986 align:middle
We've talked about the retail
sales numbers are a little bit

25:08.986 --> 25:10.826 align:middle
sluggish this month,
the unemployment,

25:10.826 --> 25:12.536 align:middle
the job growth has slowed down.

25:12.716 --> 25:14.876 align:middle
Is there more the Fed
can do to fight this?

25:14.876 --> 25:18.046 align:middle
Or is it now squarely
in the Congress's realm?

25:18.886 --> 25:19.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:19.126 --> 25:20.896 align:middle
There is more that
we can do, certainly.

25:20.896 --> 25:23.996 align:middle
We can-we can expand our,
our asset purchase programs.

25:23.996 --> 25:25.976 align:middle
We can focus them more
on the longer end.

25:25.976 --> 25:27.136 align:middle
There are a number of
options we would have

25:27.136 --> 25:30.856 align:middle
to provide more support
to the economy.

25:31.746 --> 25:35.696 align:middle
So I would-I would say,
though, that in the near term,

25:36.016 --> 25:39.796 align:middle
the help that people need isn't
just from low interest rates

25:39.796 --> 25:41.906 align:middle
that stimulates demand
over time and works

25:41.906 --> 25:42.906 align:middle
with long and variable lags.

25:42.906 --> 25:44.086 align:middle
It's really support.

25:44.326 --> 25:47.126 align:middle
As the-as-we've, we've
talked about this as all

25:47.126 --> 25:50.186 align:middle
of these government policies
trying to work together

25:50.186 --> 25:53.616 align:middle
to create a bridge across
this chasm, economic chasm

25:53.616 --> 25:55.076 align:middle
that was created
by the pandemic.

25:55.076 --> 25:59.006 align:middle
And for many Americans,
that bridge is there and,

25:59.006 --> 26:00.046 align:middle
and they're across it.

26:00.136 --> 26:01.836 align:middle
But there's a group
for, for which they,

26:01.836 --> 26:02.776 align:middle
they don't have a bridge yet.

26:02.776 --> 26:03.986 align:middle
And that's who we're
talking about here.

26:03.986 --> 26:06.776 align:middle
It's, it's the 10 million
people who lost their jobs.

26:06.776 --> 26:08.306 align:middle
It's people who may
lose their homes.

26:08.806 --> 26:12.296 align:middle
It's-you know, you see the,
the-many, many millions

26:12.296 --> 26:15.216 align:middle
of Americans are, are
waiting in food lines

26:15.216 --> 26:16.876 align:middle
in their cars these days
all over the country.

26:16.876 --> 26:18.436 align:middle
So we know there's
need out there.

26:18.436 --> 26:20.066 align:middle
We know there are
small businesses all

26:20.156 --> 26:24.126 align:middle
over the country that, that
have been basically unable

26:24.126 --> 26:27.316 align:middle
to really function, and
they're just hanging on.

26:27.386 --> 26:30.336 align:middle
So-and, and they're, they're
so critical to our economy.

26:30.336 --> 26:33.096 align:middle
So-and, by the way, now that we
can-we can kind of see the light

26:33.096 --> 26:36.656 align:middle
at the end of the tunnel, it
would be-it would be bad to see,

26:37.226 --> 26:39.656 align:middle
you know, people losing their
business, their life's work

26:39.656 --> 26:41.826 align:middle
in many cases, or even
generations' worth of work,

26:42.426 --> 26:44.556 align:middle
because they couldn't
last another few months,

26:44.556 --> 26:45.626 align:middle
which is what it amounts to.

26:45.626 --> 26:47.466 align:middle
So we have more we can do.

26:47.926 --> 26:51.416 align:middle
And we'll-I, I think more the
issue is, we're going to need

26:51.416 --> 26:53.466 align:middle
to continue to provide
support to this economy

26:53.466 --> 26:56.976 align:middle
for quite a period
of time, because the,

26:56.976 --> 26:59.086 align:middle
the economy will be
growing in expectation.

26:59.086 --> 27:01.186 align:middle
We should be growing at
a fairly healthy clip

27:01.726 --> 27:02.886 align:middle
by the second half of next year.

27:02.886 --> 27:06.356 align:middle
But it's going to be a while
before we really are back

27:06.356 --> 27:09.396 align:middle
to the levels of,
of labor market-the,

27:09.396 --> 27:12.936 align:middle
the sort of conditions in
the labor market that we had

27:13.496 --> 27:16.466 align:middle
in early this year and for much
of the last couple of years.

27:16.466 --> 27:19.756 align:middle
So that's how I think about it.

27:19.826 --> 27:21.296 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

27:21.296 --> 27:21.766 align:middle
Thank you.

27:21.766 --> 27:22.636 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:23.256 --> 27:23.936 align:middle
Thank you.

27:23.936 --> 27:24.406 align:middle
Howard Schneider.

27:24.406 --> 27:25.076 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

27:25.076 --> 27:25.706 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

27:25.706 --> 27:26.366 align:middle
Thanks for doing this.

27:26.366 --> 27:27.096 align:middle
And thanks, Michelle.

27:27.096 --> 27:28.716 align:middle
Two, two quick questions.

27:28.716 --> 27:31.966 align:middle
One, on the vaccine, do you
have, or has the Fed modeled,

27:31.966 --> 27:34.146 align:middle
sort of a working estimate

27:34.146 --> 27:37.036 align:middle
of when the U.S. might
reach something approaching

27:37.036 --> 27:39.696 align:middle
herd immunity?

27:39.696 --> 27:44.426 align:middle
And, secondly, if you could,
please connect with me the lack

27:44.426 --> 27:48.446 align:middle
of movement on the decision
to maintain the current pace

27:48.446 --> 27:51.936 align:middle
and quantities of bond
purchases with the fact

27:51.936 --> 27:54.136 align:middle
that the SEPs are
showing three years

27:54.136 --> 27:56.716 align:middle
with inflation not
reaching 2 percent.

27:56.776 --> 27:59.686 align:middle
Some might argue that
you need to do more

27:59.686 --> 28:01.386 align:middle
to start fixing those
expectations,

28:01.386 --> 28:04.806 align:middle
and to let this drift and say
"We're going to miss our target

28:04.806 --> 28:08.196 align:middle
for another three years" doesn't
show a very firm commitment

28:08.196 --> 28:09.006 align:middle
to the new framework.

28:09.266 --> 28:09.566 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:09.566 --> 28:11.696 align:middle
So, in terms of the
vaccine, yes, you know,

28:11.696 --> 28:16.086 align:middle
we do estimates of, of when the
United States would reach herd

28:16.086 --> 28:17.626 align:middle
immunity, and they're,
they're going to be similar

28:17.626 --> 28:18.616 align:middle
to what other people think.

28:18.616 --> 28:20.186 align:middle
You know, it depends
on your assumptions,

28:20.386 --> 28:23.686 align:middle
such as how many people will
actually take the vaccine

28:24.186 --> 28:26.156 align:middle
and how fast will
the rollout be.

28:26.156 --> 28:28.746 align:middle
So it's assumption based on
assumption based on assumption.

28:28.746 --> 28:33.646 align:middle
But, you know, sometime-it's
possible sometime in the middle

28:33.646 --> 28:34.776 align:middle
or second half of next year.

28:34.776 --> 28:35.976 align:middle
I'm not going to
try to be precise,

28:35.976 --> 28:38.036 align:middle
because it's just
another estimate.

28:38.036 --> 28:40.096 align:middle
You know, our people are very
good, but they're looking at,

28:40.096 --> 28:42.326 align:middle
at the same data as
other people are.

28:42.456 --> 28:46.056 align:middle
And so, you know, all the
estimates are-it depends

28:46.056 --> 28:46.966 align:middle
on what your assumptions are.

28:46.966 --> 28:49.786 align:middle
But under a normal set of
assumptions, it could happen as,

28:49.786 --> 28:51.966 align:middle
as soon as the middle
of next year.

28:52.416 --> 28:54.526 align:middle
In terms of the,
you know, inflation,

28:54.526 --> 28:56.586 align:middle
a couple things-your
second question.

28:56.956 --> 28:59.586 align:middle
You know, we're-I think
you have to be honest

28:59.586 --> 29:02.626 align:middle
with yourself about, about
inflation these days.

29:02.626 --> 29:05.566 align:middle
There are-there are significant
disinflationary pressures

29:05.566 --> 29:09.276 align:middle
around the world, and there
have been for a while,

29:09.526 --> 29:10.776 align:middle
and they persist today.

29:10.806 --> 29:14.076 align:middle
It's, is not going to be easy
to have inflation move up.

29:14.716 --> 29:16.936 align:middle
And it isn't going to be
just a question-it's going

29:16.936 --> 29:17.596 align:middle
to take some time.

29:17.596 --> 29:20.246 align:middle
It took a long time
to get inflation back

29:20.246 --> 29:22.056 align:middle
to 2 percent in the last crisis.

29:22.056 --> 29:24.306 align:middle
And, you know, we're,
we're honest with ourselves

29:24.306 --> 29:28.156 align:middle
and with you in the SEP that
even with the very high level

29:28.156 --> 29:31.136 align:middle
of accommodation that we're
providing both through low rates

29:31.136 --> 29:33.576 align:middle
and very high levels
of asset purchases,

29:34.146 --> 29:36.456 align:middle
it will take some time, because
that's what we believe the

29:36.456 --> 29:40.016 align:middle
underlying inflation
dynamics are in our economy.

29:40.126 --> 29:43.376 align:middle
And that's sort of why we're-one
reason why we're concerned

29:43.376 --> 29:45.306 align:middle
about inflation is
that we see that.

29:45.306 --> 29:48.436 align:middle
And, and that's why we have
adopted the flexible average

29:48.436 --> 29:49.686 align:middle
inflation-targeting framework.

29:49.686 --> 29:51.876 align:middle
That's why we're
aiming for an overshoot.

29:52.126 --> 29:54.396 align:middle
But we're, we're honest with
ourselves and with the public

29:54.396 --> 29:56.686 align:middle
that it will take some
time to get there.

29:56.786 --> 29:59.076 align:middle
In terms of, you know, would,
would it really speed it

29:59.076 --> 30:03.006 align:middle
up a lot to, to move
asset purchases, you know,

30:03.006 --> 30:04.976 align:middle
I don't think that would
really be-I think it's going

30:04.976 --> 30:08.196 align:middle
to take a long time
however you do it.

30:08.196 --> 30:12.756 align:middle
And, you know, we've been having
very long expansions since-in,

30:12.756 --> 30:14.846 align:middle
in the last several decades

30:14.846 --> 30:18.406 align:middle
because inflation has
not-really, the old model was,

30:18.406 --> 30:21.056 align:middle
inflation would come along
and the Fed would tighten,

30:21.056 --> 30:22.006 align:middle
and we'd have a recession.

30:22.516 --> 30:25.876 align:middle
Now inflation has been low, and
we haven't had that dynamic.

30:25.876 --> 30:29.956 align:middle
And the result has been three of
the four longest expansions in,

30:29.956 --> 30:32.096 align:middle
in modern history,
in recorded history.

30:32.096 --> 30:34.446 align:middle
So, you know, we're,
we're thinking

30:34.446 --> 30:37.676 align:middle
that this could be
another long expansion,

30:37.676 --> 30:40.486 align:middle
and that we'll keep
our-what we're saying is,

30:40.486 --> 30:44.296 align:middle
we're going to keep
policy highly accommodative

30:44.366 --> 30:46.746 align:middle
until the expansion is
well down the tracks.

30:46.896 --> 30:48.736 align:middle
And we're not going to
preemptively raise rates

30:48.736 --> 30:52.676 align:middle
until we see inflation actually
reaching 2 percent and being

30:52.676 --> 30:54.266 align:middle
on track to exceed 2 percent.

30:54.556 --> 30:56.896 align:middle
That's a very strong commitment,

30:57.076 --> 30:58.496 align:middle
and we think that's
the right place to be.

30:58.836 --> 30:59.226 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

30:59.776 --> 31:00.136 align:middle
Thank you.

31:00.136 --> 31:00.736 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:03.256 --> 31:03.696 align:middle
Thank you.

31:03.696 --> 31:04.906 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

31:05.596 --> 31:06.496 align:middle
I'm sorry, Mr. Chair.

31:06.496 --> 31:07.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:07.216 --> 31:10.846 align:middle
I'm just going to add, markets
have actually found this

31:10.946 --> 31:11.736 align:middle
fairly credible.

31:11.736 --> 31:14.546 align:middle
If you look at what-at
inflation compensation

31:14.546 --> 31:16.156 align:middle
and at the survey measures of,

31:16.846 --> 31:20.226 align:middle
of when the Fed will lift
off, everything has moved.

31:20.226 --> 31:22.006 align:middle
There have been significant
movements

31:22.006 --> 31:25.516 align:middle
since we announced the
framework in the direction

31:25.516 --> 31:28.686 align:middle
that is consistent with the-with
the framework being credible

31:28.686 --> 31:29.616 align:middle
to market participants.

31:29.616 --> 31:33.256 align:middle
So I, I don't think it's
something that-I, I mean,

31:33.256 --> 31:34.376 align:middle
I'm actually pleased by,

31:34.376 --> 31:36.106 align:middle
by the reception it's
gotten in markets.

31:36.106 --> 31:38.746 align:middle
And markets have moved in ways
that suggest it is credible.

31:38.746 --> 31:39.306 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:41.956 --> 31:42.576 align:middle
Thank you.

31:42.576 --> 31:45.536 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

31:45.536 --> 31:46.176 align:middle
Hi, thank you.

31:46.176 --> 31:47.686 align:middle
Yesterday, I guess, the Fed said

31:47.686 --> 31:50.256 align:middle
that it is joining the
international financial

31:50.256 --> 31:52.046 align:middle
greening group.

31:52.046 --> 31:54.856 align:middle
And I just wanted to
see if you could expand

31:54.856 --> 31:57.166 align:middle
on how the Fed is thinking
about climate change

31:57.166 --> 32:00.936 align:middle
and how it might affect
Fed policy going forward.

32:00.936 --> 32:03.286 align:middle
Specifically, I think Janet
Yellen drew a distinction

32:03.286 --> 32:06.706 align:middle
between considering
climate change when it comes

32:06.706 --> 32:07.856 align:middle
to financial regulation.

32:08.326 --> 32:11.506 align:middle
But how do you see that
also potentially affecting

32:11.896 --> 32:13.316 align:middle
monetary policy?

32:13.316 --> 32:15.146 align:middle
Obviously, there have
been criticisms of the Fed

32:15.146 --> 32:18.226 align:middle
for buying bonds of
oil and gas companies.

32:19.036 --> 32:21.446 align:middle
But leaving that specific
case aside, I mean,

32:21.446 --> 32:23.436 align:middle
are there-is there any way

32:23.436 --> 32:25.526 align:middle
that climate change
considerations could affect

32:25.526 --> 32:27.626 align:middle
monetary policy going forward?

32:27.856 --> 32:28.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:29.036 --> 32:31.816 align:middle
So the-you're, you're
talking about the-sorry,

32:31.816 --> 32:33.016 align:middle
the Network [of Central
Banks and Supervisors]

32:33.016 --> 32:34.236 align:middle
for Greening the
Financial System,

32:34.236 --> 32:35.516 align:middle
which we joined this week.

32:35.816 --> 32:38.126 align:middle
And I'll just-I'll just say
a couple things about that.

32:38.566 --> 32:41.496 align:middle
So, first, I'll just start
by saying that we're going

32:41.496 --> 32:43.116 align:middle
to move carefully
and thoughtfully

32:43.726 --> 32:44.886 align:middle
on developing an understanding

32:44.886 --> 32:46.846 align:middle
of how climate change
affects our work,

32:46.846 --> 32:48.236 align:middle
including the areas
you mentioned.

32:48.736 --> 32:51.116 align:middle
We're going to do so with
a great deal of engagement

32:51.116 --> 32:53.106 align:middle
with all of our external
constituencies,

32:53.106 --> 32:55.746 align:middle
including the public and
their elected representatives

32:55.746 --> 32:57.156 align:middle
who are charged with
our oversight.

32:57.596 --> 32:59.176 align:middle
We'll do it with
great transparency.

32:59.746 --> 33:02.526 align:middle
Remember that we are a
nonpolitical agency whose goals

33:02.526 --> 33:04.976 align:middle
and authorities to achieve
those goals are set by Congress.

33:05.586 --> 33:07.876 align:middle
We have great responsibilities
and strong authorities

33:08.006 --> 33:09.266 align:middle
that we'll use vigorously.

33:09.866 --> 33:12.316 align:middle
We're not the forum where all
the great issues of the day are

33:12.316 --> 33:15.556 align:middle
to be hashed out, debated,
and addressed unless and only

33:15.556 --> 33:17.676 align:middle
to the extent that those
issues are directly relevant

33:17.676 --> 33:19.776 align:middle
to our statutory goals
and are addressable

33:19.986 --> 33:21.496 align:middle
through our legal authorities.

33:21.696 --> 33:24.546 align:middle
And because we have a narrow
remit, and because we stick

33:24.546 --> 33:27.206 align:middle
to it, Congress has given us a
precious grant of independence

33:27.206 --> 33:28.416 align:middle
from direct political control.

33:28.966 --> 33:33.606 align:middle
So society's broad response to
climate change is for others

33:33.606 --> 33:35.836 align:middle
to decide-in particular,
elected leaders.

33:36.896 --> 33:38.526 align:middle
But-so let me-let me
get to your question.

33:38.956 --> 33:41.586 align:middle
What does all that mean for
an issue like climate change?

33:41.716 --> 33:43.406 align:middle
And why did we join the NGFS?

33:43.896 --> 33:46.066 align:middle
So Congress hasn't
explicitly assigned us

33:46.066 --> 33:47.806 align:middle
or other financial
regulators a role,

33:47.976 --> 33:50.026 align:middle
and we're not among the
agencies who contribute

33:50.026 --> 33:52.276 align:middle
to the National Climate
Assessment-Climate Assessment,

33:52.276 --> 33:52.726 align:middle
for example.

33:52.826 --> 33:56.026 align:middle
But climate change is
nonetheless relevant

33:56.026 --> 33:57.916 align:middle
to our existing mandates
under the law.

33:57.916 --> 33:58.726 align:middle
And let me tell you why.

33:59.436 --> 34:02.136 align:middle
One of our jobs is to regulate
and supervise banks and to look

34:02.136 --> 34:03.736 align:middle
after the stability of
the financial system.

34:03.916 --> 34:05.986 align:middle
That's a responsibility we
share with other agencies.

34:05.986 --> 34:10.386 align:middle
The public will expect that
we will-we will do that.

34:10.476 --> 34:12.566 align:middle
So we'll, we'll expect that,

34:12.566 --> 34:15.186 align:middle
that those important
institutions will be resilient

34:15.186 --> 34:18.046 align:middle
against the many resists-risks
that they fake-face:

34:18.526 --> 34:20.666 align:middle
credit risk, market
risk, cyber risk.

34:20.956 --> 34:24.406 align:middle
Climate change is an emerging
risk to financial institutions,

34:24.406 --> 34:26.086 align:middle
the financial system,
and the economy.

34:26.546 --> 34:29.786 align:middle
And we are, as so many others
are, in the very early stages

34:29.786 --> 34:32.456 align:middle
of understanding what that
means, what needs to be done

34:32.456 --> 34:33.606 align:middle
about it, and by whom.

34:34.306 --> 34:36.996 align:middle
That's why 83 central
banks have joined together

34:36.996 --> 34:39.396 align:middle
to share research and
identify best practices

34:39.426 --> 34:41.006 align:middle
in this important emerging area.

34:41.676 --> 34:45.456 align:middle
We've been attending NGFS
meetings as an observer for more

34:45.456 --> 34:47.086 align:middle
than a year, taking
part in the work.

34:47.516 --> 34:48.986 align:middle
We had discussed that
it was probably time

34:48.986 --> 34:49.836 align:middle
to join as a member.

34:50.636 --> 34:54.376 align:middle
I'll just say that the financial
system is really a global one.

34:54.376 --> 34:57.266 align:middle
It's important that we work
and discuss best practices

34:57.266 --> 35:00.296 align:middle
with peer agencies around the
world, especially in a field

35:00.296 --> 35:03.366 align:middle
where we're just beginning to
develop our, our understanding.

35:03.976 --> 35:05.676 align:middle
You asked about monetary policy.

35:06.796 --> 35:09.626 align:middle
You know, I-I'll say this.

35:10.056 --> 35:14.146 align:middle
We, we have historically
shied away strongly

35:14.596 --> 35:16.426 align:middle
from taking a role
in credit allocation.

35:16.426 --> 35:17.336 align:middle
In fact, our agreement

35:17.336 --> 35:21.236 align:middle
with Treasury during the
financial crisis from back

35:21.236 --> 35:25.476 align:middle
in 2009 or '10 says that we
will avoid credit allocation.

35:25.546 --> 35:26.936 align:middle
It's something we've
carefully avoided.

35:27.006 --> 35:30.986 align:middle
So I, I would be very
reluctant to see us move

35:30.986 --> 35:34.976 align:middle
in that direction,
picking one area

35:34.976 --> 35:37.386 align:middle
as creditworthy and another not.

35:38.076 --> 35:41.036 align:middle
So you asked about
monetary policy.

35:41.246 --> 35:44.956 align:middle
You know, it's, it's-for now,

35:44.956 --> 35:48.586 align:middle
I would say the real-the real
concern is-the real place

35:48.586 --> 35:52.696 align:middle
to focus, for me, is supervision
of financial institutions

35:52.696 --> 35:54.276 align:middle
and then financial
stability concerns.

35:54.836 --> 35:57.506 align:middle
You can see a connection there.

35:57.926 --> 36:00.406 align:middle
Monetary policy,
maximum employment,

36:00.406 --> 36:04.596 align:middle
stable prices-it's
less obvious to me.

36:04.596 --> 36:08.426 align:middle
I can-I can, you know-I
can make the argument,

36:08.426 --> 36:09.376 align:middle
but it's less obvious to me

36:09.376 --> 36:12.486 align:middle
that those should be first-order
things that we would look

36:12.486 --> 36:13.666 align:middle
at in connection
with climate change.

36:13.666 --> 36:15.746 align:middle
But I think there's-I think
there's work to be done

36:15.746 --> 36:18.856 align:middle
to understand the connection
between climate change

36:18.856 --> 36:21.256 align:middle
and the strength and
resilience of financial markets

36:21.656 --> 36:22.786 align:middle
and financial institutions.

36:22.786 --> 36:25.046 align:middle
I think that work is
in its early days.

36:25.596 --> 36:27.076 align:middle
And, again, we'll be careful.

36:27.076 --> 36:30.786 align:middle
We'll be thorough
and transparent and,

36:30.826 --> 36:33.416 align:middle
and engage with the
public on all that.

36:34.456 --> 36:36.096 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

36:36.096 --> 36:36.476 align:middle
Thank you.

36:36.956 --> 36:37.306 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

36:37.306 --> 36:37.836 align:middle
Thank you.

36:37.836 --> 36:38.646 align:middle
Rachel Siegel.

36:38.646 --> 36:43.596 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

36:43.736 --> 36:44.476 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

36:44.476 --> 36:45.886 align:middle
Thanks very much for
taking my question.

36:45.886 --> 36:46.776 align:middle
And thank you, Michelle.

36:47.076 --> 36:50.326 align:middle
I wanted to ask specifically
about state and local aid.

36:50.326 --> 36:52.986 align:middle
Just as one example,
the governor

36:52.986 --> 36:56.236 align:middle
of Illinois announced
roughly $700 million

36:56.236 --> 37:00.076 align:middle
in early anticipated budget cuts
to close the budget deficit,

37:00.076 --> 37:02.866 align:middle
and the latest bipartisan
stimulus proposal that's being

37:02.866 --> 37:05.636 align:middle
debated this week seems
to be leaving out state

37:05.636 --> 37:07.506 align:middle
and local aid at this point.

37:07.506 --> 37:09.596 align:middle
And I'm just curious if
you can give some detail

37:09.596 --> 37:12.406 align:middle
about the damage that these
budget deficits or the lack

37:12.406 --> 37:15.536 align:middle
of aid specifically to state
and local governments will have

37:15.536 --> 37:17.506 align:middle
on the recovery and,
specifically,

37:17.506 --> 37:20.736 align:middle
with the municipal lending
facility set to expire,

37:20.736 --> 37:22.276 align:middle
what more the Fed
might be able to do

37:22.276 --> 37:23.846 align:middle
to fill that specific hole.

37:23.956 --> 37:24.726 align:middle
Thanks very much.

37:24.726 --> 37:25.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:25.126 --> 37:30.856 align:middle
Okay. So the state
and local governments,

37:30.856 --> 37:35.386 align:middle
they provide important critical
services: safety, fire, police,

37:35.616 --> 37:37.006 align:middle
health, all kinds
of things like that.

37:37.006 --> 37:39.196 align:middle
They're, they're really
involved in people's lives,

37:39.196 --> 37:41.586 align:middle
state and local governments
are, to, to a large extent.

37:42.466 --> 37:47.416 align:middle
The decision whether to, to
provide more fiscal support

37:47.416 --> 37:49.296 align:middle
to them is entirely in
the hands of Congress.

37:49.636 --> 37:51.986 align:middle
And, you know, they're in the
middle of these discussions,

37:51.986 --> 37:54.106 align:middle
and, and those, those are
issues for them to decide.

37:54.466 --> 37:57.016 align:middle
I would say that the
picture is mixed.

37:57.466 --> 38:00.406 align:middle
What's happened is,
if you're a state

38:00.676 --> 38:04.266 align:middle
that has a significant
exposure to tourism

38:04.266 --> 38:08.046 align:middle
or to extracting energy from
the ground-oil from the ground,

38:08.746 --> 38:13.176 align:middle
or gas-you are probably, at
least in those industries,

38:13.176 --> 38:14.776 align:middle
feeling a significant
loss of revenue.

38:15.246 --> 38:19.706 align:middle
A number of other states have
been surprised on the upside,

38:19.826 --> 38:22.056 align:middle
where-and that's
because goods sales,

38:22.056 --> 38:25.816 align:middle
for example-property taxes
are-don't move much year

38:25.816 --> 38:26.316 align:middle
to year.

38:26.416 --> 38:29.846 align:middle
And, you know, the
kind of sales taxes

38:29.846 --> 38:33.146 align:middle
on goods-goods sales
have been very high.

38:33.246 --> 38:35.956 align:middle
Income has been more
or less replaced by,

38:35.956 --> 38:39.536 align:middle
by the CARES Act-fully replaced,
more than, in many cases.

38:40.266 --> 38:44.486 align:middle
So the concerns that we
had at the very beginning

38:44.486 --> 38:47.066 align:middle
of really serious,
deep shortfalls

38:47.636 --> 38:50.156 align:middle
and massive budget cuts
on the part of state

38:50.156 --> 38:53.836 align:middle
and local governments have
not yet-have not yet occurred.

38:54.026 --> 38:57.456 align:middle
What we're seeing is, is that
it's different state to state.

38:58.116 --> 39:01.826 align:middle
And some states are having
significant difficulties,

39:01.956 --> 39:03.216 align:middle
others not so much.

39:03.716 --> 39:07.256 align:middle
The real concern, though,
that still emerges is,

39:07.586 --> 39:09.896 align:middle
state and local governments
are very large employers,

39:10.026 --> 39:11.526 align:middle
and-one of the largest.

39:12.256 --> 39:18.916 align:middle
And so far, since the pandemic
began, employment has dropped

39:18.916 --> 39:22.986 align:middle
by 1.3 million in-of state
and local government.

39:22.986 --> 39:25.746 align:middle
So it's, it's a very large
number of people to be-to be

39:25.746 --> 39:27.846 align:middle
out of work from
just that one source.

39:27.846 --> 39:29.296 align:middle
It's actually significantly more

39:29.296 --> 39:31.606 align:middle
than lost their jobs during
the Global Financial Crisis.

39:32.416 --> 39:36.186 align:middle
So that is-that is a
significant-a significant part

39:36.186 --> 39:38.996 align:middle
of that group is,
is in education.

39:39.766 --> 39:42.686 align:middle
So when the schools
reopen, a significant part

39:42.686 --> 39:44.436 align:middle
of that 1.3 million
would go back to work.

39:44.436 --> 39:45.726 align:middle
Nonetheless, it's a concern.

39:45.726 --> 39:49.426 align:middle
We're, we're watching carefully
to understand why that-why

39:49.426 --> 39:51.136 align:middle
that many people
have been let go

39:51.136 --> 39:52.596 align:middle
and what are-really
are the sources.

39:52.596 --> 39:54.696 align:middle
So we're monitoring
it carefully,

39:54.696 --> 39:56.166 align:middle
and it's a mixed picture.

39:56.836 --> 39:59.566 align:middle
And I, I just have to leave
the question of what to do

39:59.566 --> 40:01.706 align:middle
with fiscal policy
on this to Congress.

40:01.986 --> 40:02.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:05.186 --> 40:06.016 align:middle
Thank you.

40:06.016 --> 40:06.916 align:middle
James Politi.

40:07.226 --> 40:08.476 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

40:08.866 --> 40:10.296 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

40:10.296 --> 40:11.866 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell.

40:12.226 --> 40:15.686 align:middle
You and the Fed have
consistently said

40:15.756 --> 40:17.886 align:middle
that the Fed is ready
to provide the economy

40:17.886 --> 40:21.696 align:middle
with more monetary support
if needed and really stressed

40:21.696 --> 40:24.146 align:middle
to policymakers generally
that the dangers

40:24.146 --> 40:28.186 align:middle
of not doing enough outweigh
the risks of doing too much

40:28.346 --> 40:31.136 align:middle
in a situation where there's so
much suffering in the economy.

40:31.136 --> 40:35.746 align:middle
The guidance today sort
of cements asset purchases

40:35.746 --> 40:39.396 align:middle
for a longer period of time but
is not a big new easing step.

40:39.436 --> 40:43.646 align:middle
Why was now, with the short-term
outlook deteriorating due

40:43.646 --> 40:47.196 align:middle
to the new coronavirus
surges, not the moment

40:47.256 --> 40:49.336 align:middle
for a big new easing step?

40:49.336 --> 40:53.226 align:middle
And is it because of the
medium-term outlook improving?

40:53.466 --> 40:56.596 align:middle
Or is it because the Fed just
doesn't have the capacity

40:56.596 --> 41:00.196 align:middle
to sort of build that
bridge over the next four

41:00.196 --> 41:02.866 align:middle
to five months for the-for
the people who are struggling?

41:03.596 --> 41:04.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:04.846 --> 41:09.806 align:middle
Right. So I, I could go back,
James, to what I said earlier,

41:09.806 --> 41:11.526 align:middle
which is, this is-this
is a very,

41:11.526 --> 41:13.146 align:middle
very large asset
purchase program.

41:13.146 --> 41:14.936 align:middle
It's providing a
tremendous amount of,

41:15.586 --> 41:17.486 align:middle
of support for the economy.

41:17.636 --> 41:19.336 align:middle
If you look at the
interest-sensitive parts

41:19.336 --> 41:21.116 align:middle
of the economy, they're
performing very well.

41:21.466 --> 41:24.036 align:middle
The parts that are not
performing well are not

41:24.036 --> 41:25.416 align:middle
struggling from high
interest rates.

41:25.446 --> 41:29.356 align:middle
They're struggling from exposure
to, to COVID, in a sense.

41:29.356 --> 41:31.196 align:middle
These are-these are
the businesses that,

41:31.196 --> 41:32.826 align:middle
that are really hit hard

41:32.826 --> 41:36.316 align:middle
by people's reluctance
to gather closely.

41:36.436 --> 41:40.996 align:middle
So, so we, we do have
the ability to-you know,

41:40.996 --> 41:45.136 align:middle
to buy more bonds or to
buy longer-term bonds.

41:45.186 --> 41:47.046 align:middle
And, and we may-we may use it.

41:47.046 --> 41:48.526 align:middle
I'm not saying we
won't-we won't use that.

41:48.526 --> 41:50.516 align:middle
It may well come
to, to using that.

41:51.206 --> 41:54.876 align:middle
But also, I would remind-I
would also note, though,

41:55.326 --> 41:57.706 align:middle
monetary policy works with
"long and variable lags,"

41:57.846 --> 41:58.856 align:middle
is the famous statement.

41:59.286 --> 42:02.816 align:middle
So we think that the big effects
from monetary policy are,

42:03.586 --> 42:05.066 align:middle
you know, months and
months into the future.

42:05.576 --> 42:09.466 align:middle
So this looks like a-you
know, it looks like a time

42:09.466 --> 42:11.266 align:middle
when what is really
needed is fiscal policy.

42:11.266 --> 42:14.246 align:middle
And, and that's why it is, is
a very positive thing that,

42:14.796 --> 42:15.556 align:middle
that we're getting that.

42:16.036 --> 42:18.646 align:middle
So we, we remain open
to doing-you know, to,

42:18.946 --> 42:23.056 align:middle
to either increasing the
size of our asset purchases,

42:23.376 --> 42:24.816 align:middle
if that turns out
to be appropriate,

42:24.816 --> 42:26.756 align:middle
or to just moving
the maturities,

42:27.156 --> 42:28.776 align:middle
moving to buying
longer maturities,

42:28.776 --> 42:31.256 align:middle
because that had-that also
increases accommodation

42:31.256 --> 42:33.636 align:middle
by taking more duration
risk out of the market.

42:34.336 --> 42:36.286 align:middle
But we think our current
stance is appropriate.

42:36.496 --> 42:38.996 align:middle
And we think that our-you
know, that our, our guidance

42:38.996 --> 42:42.376 align:middle
on asset purchases today
will also provide support

42:42.376 --> 42:43.736 align:middle
to the economy over time.

42:44.346 --> 42:45.786 align:middle
Again, what we've-what
we've done is,

42:45.786 --> 42:48.456 align:middle
we've laid out a path
whereby we're going

42:48.456 --> 42:51.756 align:middle
to keep monetary policy highly
accommodative for a long time,

42:51.756 --> 42:56.576 align:middle
really until-really until we
reach very close to our goals,

42:56.966 --> 42:57.876 align:middle
which is not, you know,

42:57.876 --> 42:59.776 align:middle
not really the way it's
been done in the past.

42:59.776 --> 43:02.106 align:middle
So that's, that's
providing significant support

43:02.106 --> 43:03.106 align:middle
for the economy now.

43:03.826 --> 43:06.096 align:middle
We don't think the economy
suffers from a lack of,

43:06.176 --> 43:08.246 align:middle
of highly accommodative
financial conditions.

43:08.246 --> 43:11.836 align:middle
We think it's suffering from
the pandemic and people wanting

43:11.836 --> 43:14.406 align:middle
to not engage in certain
kinds of economic activity.

43:15.606 --> 43:17.386 align:middle
And we expect that,
that with the virus,

43:17.386 --> 43:20.386 align:middle
that that will improve-that
condition will improve.

43:20.386 --> 43:23.776 align:middle
Nonetheless, again, we will-are
prepared to use our tools,

43:23.776 --> 43:27.586 align:middle
and we will use them at such
time and in such amounts

43:27.586 --> 43:29.316 align:middle
as we-as we think
would, would help.

43:29.406 --> 43:29.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:33.476 --> 43:33.856 align:middle
Thank you.

43:33.856 --> 43:34.946 align:middle
Scott Horsley, NPR.

43:34.946 --> 43:35.626 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

43:38.726 --> 43:40.366 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

43:40.366 --> 43:43.126 align:middle
Some forecasters have
suggested that there is a lot

43:43.126 --> 43:47.936 align:middle
of pent-up demand for travel and
entertainment and, and services

43:47.936 --> 43:52.016 align:middle
and the like that might stop
being pent up in a hurry

43:52.446 --> 43:53.726 align:middle
if we do get to a point

43:53.726 --> 43:56.376 align:middle
where the vaccine is
widely distributed,

43:56.376 --> 43:59.836 align:middle
and that the beaten-down service
sector might have trouble

43:59.836 --> 44:02.926 align:middle
meeting that demand
in, in a hurry.

44:03.596 --> 44:07.386 align:middle
How might that affect
inflation, in your mind?

44:07.386 --> 44:10.106 align:middle
And would that be just
a transient problem or,

44:10.106 --> 44:12.016 align:middle
or something that might
warrant closer scrutiny?

44:12.426 --> 44:13.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:13.636 --> 44:15.816 align:middle
So that, that has
all the markings

44:15.816 --> 44:19.276 align:middle
of a transient increase
in the price level.

44:19.276 --> 44:22.346 align:middle
So you can imagine that
as people really want

44:22.346 --> 44:24.976 align:middle
to travel again-let's say,
you know, that airfare,

44:25.116 --> 44:27.346 align:middle
airfares-I'm just imagining
this, right?-that they go up.

44:27.346 --> 44:30.696 align:middle
But what inflation is, is
a process whereby they go

44:30.696 --> 44:34.866 align:middle
up year upon year
upon year upon year.

44:35.006 --> 44:38.536 align:middle
And if-given the inflation
dynamics that we've had

44:38.746 --> 44:42.136 align:middle
over the last several
decades, just a single,

44:42.336 --> 44:46.726 align:middle
single sort of price-level
increase has not resulted

44:46.726 --> 44:49.656 align:middle
in ongoing price-level
increases.

44:50.106 --> 44:53.236 align:middle
And that, that was-the
problem back in the 1970s was,

44:53.356 --> 44:55.146 align:middle
it was the combination
of two things.

44:55.576 --> 44:59.026 align:middle
One, when unemployment went
down and resources got tight,

44:59.446 --> 45:00.616 align:middle
prices started going up.

45:01.116 --> 45:03.826 align:middle
But the second problem
was that that-that

45:03.826 --> 45:05.676 align:middle
that increase was persistent,

45:05.676 --> 45:06.906 align:middle
there was a level
of persistence.

45:06.906 --> 45:10.406 align:middle
So if prices went up
6 percent this year,

45:10.406 --> 45:11.616 align:middle
they'd go up 6 percent
next year,

45:11.616 --> 45:13.416 align:middle
because people would
internalize.

45:13.416 --> 45:14.986 align:middle
I mean, really, really,
that's what happens is,

45:14.986 --> 45:18.066 align:middle
people internalize that they can
raise prices, and that it's okay

45:18.066 --> 45:20.626 align:middle
to pay prices that are
going up at that rate.

45:20.626 --> 45:23.486 align:middle
So that was the inflation-those
are the inflation dynamics

45:24.366 --> 45:25.186 align:middle
of that era.

45:25.546 --> 45:27.356 align:middle
Those dynamics are
not in place anymore.

45:27.356 --> 45:29.956 align:middle
There-the connection
between low unemployment

45:29.956 --> 45:32.906 align:middle
or other resource
utilization and inflation is

45:32.906 --> 45:34.206 align:middle
so much weaker than it was.

45:34.206 --> 45:37.356 align:middle
It's still there, but it's
a-it's a faint heartbeat

45:37.356 --> 45:38.186 align:middle
compared to what it was.

45:38.596 --> 45:40.066 align:middle
And the persistence
of inflation,

45:40.066 --> 45:44.306 align:middle
if you-if you get a-for
example, oil prices go up,

45:44.396 --> 45:47.256 align:middle
and that'll send a temporary
shock through the economy.

45:47.536 --> 45:50.976 align:middle
The persistence of that
into, into inflation

45:50.976 --> 45:52.576 align:middle
over time is just not there.

45:52.576 --> 45:56.176 align:middle
So they-what you describe
may happen, and, of course,

45:56.176 --> 45:57.556 align:middle
we would watch it
very carefully.

45:57.556 --> 46:02.396 align:middle
We, we understand that we will
always be learning new things

46:02.396 --> 46:05.946 align:middle
from the economy about how it
will behave in certain cases.

46:05.986 --> 46:08.666 align:middle
But I would-I would expect,
though, going in that

46:08.666 --> 46:12.396 align:middle
that would be a one time price
increase rather than an increase

46:12.476 --> 46:14.626 align:middle
in underlying inflation
that would be persistent.

46:14.736 --> 46:15.656 align:middle
SCOTT HORSLEY.

46:15.776 --> 46:17.556 align:middle
So not the kind of thing
that the Fed would,

46:17.616 --> 46:19.946 align:middle
would be trigger-happy
to, to- CHAIR POWELL.

46:19.946 --> 46:20.446 align:middle
No. SCOTT HORSLEY.

46:20.446 --> 46:20.986 align:middle
-respond to?

46:20.986 --> 46:21.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:21.876 --> 46:22.746 align:middle
No, no. Definitely not.

46:22.866 --> 46:23.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:25.036 --> 46:25.666 align:middle
Thank you.

46:25.666 --> 46:26.356 align:middle
Michael Derby.

46:26.766 --> 46:29.926 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

46:30.076 --> 46:31.836 align:middle
[Inaudible]-question.

46:31.836 --> 46:35.076 align:middle
But what I wanted to ask
was, you know, given the size

46:35.076 --> 46:37.566 align:middle
of all the government
borrowing and, you know,

46:37.566 --> 46:40.196 align:middle
different regulatory changes
in the financial sector,

46:40.196 --> 46:43.406 align:middle
do you think that the
Treasury market, long run,

46:43.406 --> 46:45.666 align:middle
can operate smoothly
without some sort

46:45.666 --> 46:50.546 align:middle
of active Fed presence-you
know, buying Treasury debt?

46:50.546 --> 46:55.146 align:middle
You know, I'm referring back
to [inaudible] Randal Quarles.

46:55.146 --> 46:56.656 align:middle
He had some questions about it.

46:56.656 --> 46:58.366 align:middle
So I wanted to know where
you stood on that issue.

46:58.366 --> 47:00.166 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:00.306 --> 47:02.436 align:middle
Right. So-you were
breaking up a little.

47:02.436 --> 47:05.836 align:middle
I think-I think I got
the sense of it, though.

47:05.836 --> 47:09.106 align:middle
Yes, I, I don't think it's
at all a foregone conclusion

47:09.106 --> 47:12.436 align:middle
that there needs to be a
permanent Fed presence.

47:12.436 --> 47:14.206 align:middle
And we certainly don't-you know,

47:14.206 --> 47:16.176 align:middle
that's not something we're
planning on or intending.

47:16.666 --> 47:19.346 align:middle
Right now we're buying assets
because it's a-you know,

47:19.346 --> 47:23.126 align:middle
it's a-it's a time when
the economy needs highly

47:23.126 --> 47:24.306 align:middle
accommodative monetary policy,

47:24.306 --> 47:26.766 align:middle
and we think our asset
purchases are, you know,

47:26.766 --> 47:29.376 align:middle
one of the main delivery
mechanisms for that,

47:29.546 --> 47:30.966 align:middle
the size of the balance sheet.

47:30.966 --> 47:34.696 align:middle
You know, there's
lots of demand for,

47:34.826 --> 47:38.406 align:middle
for U.S. Treasury paper
all-from all over the world.

47:38.996 --> 47:43.346 align:middle
And I, I think we need to be
thoughtful about the structure

47:43.346 --> 47:46.126 align:middle
of the Treasury market and
look at ways to make sure

47:46.126 --> 47:48.856 align:middle
that the capacity is
there for it to be handled

47:48.856 --> 47:49.716 align:middle
by the private sector.

47:49.716 --> 47:53.396 align:middle
And there's, there's quite a lot
of work going on on that front.

47:53.396 --> 47:56.966 align:middle
But I, I don't presume at all
that, that this is something

47:56.966 --> 48:00.246 align:middle
that needs to be-where the Fed
needs to be in there at all.

48:00.246 --> 48:01.836 align:middle
I, I would think that
this should be handled

48:01.836 --> 48:03.236 align:middle
by the private sector
and can be.

48:03.376 --> 48:06.246 align:middle
Institutions need to be
able to hold this paper.

48:07.136 --> 48:09.666 align:middle
And there may be-there may
be a central clearing angle

48:09.926 --> 48:11.876 align:middle
that would, you know,
net a lot of risk.

48:11.946 --> 48:13.266 align:middle
That's, that's yet to be proven.

48:13.266 --> 48:14.846 align:middle
There are a lot of things that
are being looked at right now.

48:15.406 --> 48:15.706 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

48:15.706 --> 48:16.126 align:middle
Thank you.

48:16.486 --> 48:17.436 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

48:17.436 --> 48:18.076 align:middle
Thank you.

48:18.476 --> 48:19.086 align:middle
Hannah Lang.

48:19.086 --> 48:19.426 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

48:23.116 --> 48:26.516 align:middle
Hi, hi. Thanks so much
for taking our questions.

48:26.756 --> 48:27.806 align:middle
So, you know, as you know,

48:27.806 --> 48:30.236 align:middle
the pandemic has really
disproportionately impacted

48:30.236 --> 48:32.506 align:middle
small and medium-sized
businesses.

48:32.506 --> 48:35.446 align:middle
And the Fed launched a few
efforts this year to-through its

48:35.446 --> 48:38.586 align:middle
13(3) programs to reach
out to those companies.

48:38.586 --> 48:40.506 align:middle
But, you know, considering
that some

48:40.506 --> 48:42.686 align:middle
of those programs are
ending, is there anything else

48:42.686 --> 48:44.446 align:middle
that the Fed's actions can do

48:44.446 --> 48:47.046 align:middle
to provide support
to those companies?

48:47.046 --> 48:49.626 align:middle
Or is that solely
Congress's domain?

48:49.706 --> 48:50.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:50.556 --> 48:51.586 align:middle
I think the main thing

48:51.586 --> 48:55.226 align:middle
that those companies need is
a-is a robust recovery-a strong,

48:55.226 --> 48:56.116 align:middle
robust recovery.

48:56.796 --> 48:59.136 align:middle
And so we, we contribute to that

48:59.216 --> 49:03.026 align:middle
through highly accommodative
monetary policy,

49:03.026 --> 49:04.636 align:middle
through accommodative
financial conditions

49:04.636 --> 49:06.296 align:middle
that are supporting
economic activity.

49:06.986 --> 49:09.996 align:middle
Health authorities are
contributing to that through,

49:10.176 --> 49:14.756 align:middle
through, you know, management of
the spread of the virus and now

49:14.756 --> 49:17.446 align:middle
through vaccines and the
delivery of those vaccines.

49:17.956 --> 49:20.036 align:middle
And Congress contributes,
contributes to that

49:20.646 --> 49:23.386 align:middle
by helping them make it through
this very difficult time.

49:23.686 --> 49:25.986 align:middle
And, and I think
my understanding is

49:25.986 --> 49:28.206 align:middle
that there's support
for small businesses in,

49:28.486 --> 49:30.886 align:middle
in what's being discussed
on Capitol Hill.

49:30.886 --> 49:31.726 align:middle
I would certainly think

49:31.726 --> 49:34.446 align:middle
that would be something
well worth looking at.

49:34.776 --> 49:37.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:37.956 --> 49:38.396 align:middle
Thank you.

49:38.396 --> 49:39.126 align:middle
Paul La Monica.

49:39.126 --> 49:39.516 align:middle
PAUL LA MONICA.

49:41.396 --> 49:43.206 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell-hi,
Chair Powell.

49:43.206 --> 49:45.626 align:middle
I was wondering if you've
had any conversations

49:45.626 --> 49:48.876 align:middle
yet with any members of the
incoming Biden Administration,

49:48.876 --> 49:50.586 align:middle
particularly Janet Yellen,

49:50.586 --> 49:54.476 align:middle
who obviously you can share
some similar experience

49:54.476 --> 49:55.396 align:middle
in running the Fed.

49:55.396 --> 49:59.796 align:middle
So just curious to get your
thoughts on how the relationship

49:59.796 --> 50:03.116 align:middle
with Treasury and the new
Administration might differ

50:03.116 --> 50:04.726 align:middle
than the past couple of years.

50:04.726 --> 50:06.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:06.596 --> 50:10.186 align:middle
So, you know, we've
had a-and I've had sort

50:10.246 --> 50:15.576 align:middle
of the typical meetings with the
transition team for Treasury.

50:15.576 --> 50:16.916 align:middle
They've met with
quite a lot of people

50:17.576 --> 50:19.316 align:middle
in our agency and
other agencies.

50:19.546 --> 50:22.786 align:middle
And, really, it's about
learning what we do.

50:23.116 --> 50:27.756 align:middle
And I, I really have only
spoken to former Chair Yellen

50:28.316 --> 50:32.836 align:middle
to congratulate her on being
nominated and just to say

50:32.836 --> 50:34.866 align:middle
that I look forward
to working with her.

50:34.866 --> 50:36.526 align:middle
You know, I did work
very closely with her

50:36.526 --> 50:39.096 align:middle
for five years before she left

50:39.096 --> 50:41.516 align:middle
and have stayed in
touch, you know.

50:41.636 --> 50:42.686 align:middle
So I did that.

50:42.716 --> 50:45.566 align:middle
But I have not discussed,
you know, policy with her.

50:45.626 --> 50:47.546 align:middle
And I, I'm not going to do
that until she's confirmed.

50:47.546 --> 50:48.166 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

50:51.466 --> 50:52.336 align:middle
Great, thank you.

50:52.336 --> 50:55.006 align:middle
Jeff Cox. JEFF COX.

50:55.306 --> 50:57.436 align:middle
Yes. Chair, thank you
for taking the question.

50:57.436 --> 51:00.626 align:middle
You've been asked this question
before in various forms.

51:00.626 --> 51:04.006 align:middle
I'm going to try it a
little bit differently now.

51:04.006 --> 51:06.406 align:middle
The equity market and
the bond market seem

51:06.406 --> 51:08.726 align:middle
to be telling two different
stories about where,

51:08.726 --> 51:12.286 align:middle
where things are heading.

51:12.396 --> 51:15.316 align:middle
We [inaudible] on
the stock market,

51:15.316 --> 51:17.236 align:middle
bond yields remain very low.

51:17.506 --> 51:20.646 align:middle
Does that concern you at all?

51:20.646 --> 51:23.206 align:middle
Are, are you getting
any more concerned

51:23.206 --> 51:25.166 align:middle
about asset valuations in,

51:25.166 --> 51:27.816 align:middle
in light of the highly
accommodative Fed policies?

51:28.846 --> 51:29.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:29.196 --> 51:31.376 align:middle
You, you were breaking up,
but I, I think I got that.

51:31.376 --> 51:32.346 align:middle
I think I got it.

51:32.346 --> 51:35.546 align:middle
So, you know, financial
stability-we,

51:35.656 --> 51:37.626 align:middle
we look at a broad
range of things.

51:37.626 --> 51:40.826 align:middle
We actually have a framework
so that we can, you know,

51:40.826 --> 51:43.956 align:middle
be-evaluate changes in
financial stability over time,

51:43.956 --> 51:46.096 align:middle
and so that the public can
evaluate whether we're doing a

51:46.096 --> 51:47.116 align:middle
job-a good job at it.

51:47.116 --> 51:48.826 align:middle
We-so what do we look at?

51:48.826 --> 51:50.816 align:middle
Asset prices is one
thing that we look at.

51:51.106 --> 51:53.466 align:middle
And, you know, we published
a report a few weeks ago

51:53.466 --> 51:55.426 align:middle
on that-maybe it
was month or so ago.

51:55.506 --> 51:59.046 align:middle
Anyway. And I think you'll
find a mixed bag there.

51:59.046 --> 52:00.576 align:middle
It depends-with equities,

52:00.576 --> 52:02.626 align:middle
it depends on whether
you're looking at P/Es

52:02.626 --> 52:04.146 align:middle
or whether you're
looking at the premium

52:04.146 --> 52:06.746 align:middle
over risk-free-over
the risk-free return.

52:07.516 --> 52:09.726 align:middle
If you look at P/Es, they're
historically high, but,

52:09.726 --> 52:10.416 align:middle
you know, in a world

52:10.416 --> 52:14.636 align:middle
where the-where the risk-free
rate is sustain-is going

52:14.636 --> 52:18.806 align:middle
to be low for a sustained
period, the equity premium,

52:18.876 --> 52:21.276 align:middle
which is really the reward you
get for taking equity risk,

52:21.856 --> 52:23.016 align:middle
would be what you'd look at.

52:23.016 --> 52:27.386 align:middle
And that's not at, at incredibly
low levels, which would mean

52:27.386 --> 52:28.986 align:middle
that they're not
overpriced in that sense.

52:29.126 --> 52:32.676 align:middle
Admittedly, P/Es are high,
but that's-you know, that's,

52:32.746 --> 52:34.586 align:middle
that's maybe not as
relevant in a world

52:34.586 --> 52:36.996 align:middle
where we think the 10-year
Treasury is going to be lower

52:36.996 --> 52:38.166 align:middle
than it's been historically,

52:38.376 --> 52:39.696 align:middle
from a-from a return
perspective.

52:40.526 --> 52:45.166 align:middle
You know, we look at-we also
look at borrowing-leverage

52:45.166 --> 52:46.556 align:middle
of financial institutions.

52:46.606 --> 52:48.866 align:middle
We spent 10 years and the
banks spent 10 years building

52:48.866 --> 52:49.676 align:middle
up their capital.

52:50.526 --> 52:53.126 align:middle
So far, they've been a source
of strength through this crisis,

52:53.126 --> 52:54.506 align:middle
and their capital
has held up well.

52:55.226 --> 52:56.126 align:middle
We look at leverage

52:56.126 --> 52:58.116 align:middle
in the nonfinancial
sector-that's households

52:58.116 --> 52:59.236 align:middle
and nonfinancial corporates.

52:59.236 --> 53:01.226 align:middle
Nonfinancial corporate
leverage is high.

53:01.926 --> 53:02.946 align:middle
We've been watching that.

53:02.996 --> 53:04.516 align:middle
But, you know, rates
are really low.

53:04.516 --> 53:08.896 align:middle
So-and so companies
have been able

53:08.896 --> 53:12.106 align:middle
to handle their debt loads
even in weak periods,

53:12.106 --> 53:13.756 align:middle
because rates are-rates
are quite low.

53:13.756 --> 53:15.486 align:middle
Your interest payments are low.

53:15.886 --> 53:18.206 align:middle
Defaults and downgrades
have, have declined

53:18.206 --> 53:19.186 align:middle
since earlier in the year.

53:19.536 --> 53:21.516 align:middle
Households came into
this very strong,

53:21.836 --> 53:24.086 align:middle
and there certainly
has been a hit there

53:24.496 --> 53:26.006 align:middle
for people who are unemployed.

53:26.136 --> 53:27.946 align:middle
But, you know, with
the CARES Act,

53:28.556 --> 53:30.646 align:middle
Congress replaced a
lot of lost income.

53:30.776 --> 53:32.296 align:middle
You know, it's very
important that,

53:32.856 --> 53:34.436 align:middle
that the economy
gets strong again.

53:34.436 --> 53:35.806 align:middle
I mean, the, the ultimate thing

53:35.806 --> 53:38.816 align:middle
to support financial
stability is a strong economy.

53:39.236 --> 53:43.286 align:middle
The last thing is, is
really funding markets.

53:43.546 --> 53:47.246 align:middle
We found that there was a lot of
unstable funding for companies,

53:47.246 --> 53:48.546 align:middle
particularly financial
companies.

53:48.966 --> 53:51.956 align:middle
And that's, that's down to a-to
a very low level these days.

53:51.956 --> 53:53.966 align:middle
So the broad financial
stability picture is,

53:54.546 --> 53:56.016 align:middle
is kind of mixed, I would say.

53:56.566 --> 54:00.176 align:middle
I would say, you know, asset
prices are, are a little high

54:00.176 --> 54:01.686 align:middle
in that metric and in my view.

54:01.686 --> 54:03.466 align:middle
But, overall, you
have a mixed picture.

54:03.466 --> 54:07.996 align:middle
You, you don't have, you know,
a lot of red flags on that.

54:08.896 --> 54:10.826 align:middle
So-and it's-again,
it's something

54:10.826 --> 54:16.236 align:middle
that we monitor essentially
ongoing, almost daily.

54:16.236 --> 54:18.526 align:middle
You know, we're, we're
monitoring these prices

54:18.526 --> 54:22.646 align:middle
for that and, and have published
our, our framework, and,

54:22.646 --> 54:24.736 align:middle
you know, we'll be
held accountable

54:24.736 --> 54:26.336 align:middle
for what we saw and
what we missed.

54:26.436 --> 54:28.666 align:middle
So we work very hard at it.

54:30.256 --> 54:30.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:30.896 --> 54:31.296 align:middle
Thank you.

54:31.296 --> 54:33.276 align:middle
And for the last question
we'll go to Michael McKee.

54:33.276 --> 54:33.776 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

54:35.566 --> 54:37.916 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I have
a couple of questions

54:37.916 --> 54:40.216 align:middle
about the fiscal
support for the economy.

54:40.216 --> 54:43.156 align:middle
This year's budget
deficit is $3.1 trillion.

54:43.156 --> 54:45.526 align:middle
And Republicans have argued
they don't want to spend more,

54:45.846 --> 54:47.136 align:middle
because we can't afford it.

54:47.316 --> 54:48.966 align:middle
So I'm wondering if
you can make the case

54:48.966 --> 54:50.956 align:middle
for how much we can afford.

54:50.956 --> 54:55.336 align:middle
At what point do deficits and
the debt start to have an impact

54:55.336 --> 54:57.986 align:middle
on the economy or
on interest rates?

54:57.986 --> 55:01.396 align:middle
And how would we know
when we get there?

55:01.736 --> 55:02.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

55:02.626 --> 55:05.686 align:middle
So people who-people who run
for elected office and win,

55:06.026 --> 55:07.366 align:middle
they're the ones-their
reward is they get

55:07.366 --> 55:09.106 align:middle
to make those very
difficult decisions.

55:09.106 --> 55:12.106 align:middle
And-you know, so
we're not charged

55:12.106 --> 55:15.656 align:middle
with providing fiscal
advice to, to Congress.

55:15.716 --> 55:19.706 align:middle
But I, I would just say, as
a general-as a general rule,

55:20.096 --> 55:23.566 align:middle
it is important to be on
a sustainable fiscal path.

55:24.206 --> 55:27.646 align:middle
For my way of thinking and
many others', the time to focus

55:27.646 --> 55:29.096 align:middle
on that is when the
economy is strong

55:29.836 --> 55:33.766 align:middle
and when unemployment is low
and taxes are-you know, are,

55:33.866 --> 55:38.106 align:middle
are pouring in, and there's,
there's room to get on-get

55:38.106 --> 55:41.296 align:middle
on a sustainable path, because
the economy's really doing well.

55:41.556 --> 55:45.716 align:middle
You're still now in the-in
the, you know, some part of,

55:45.716 --> 55:47.236 align:middle
of an economic crisis.

55:47.236 --> 55:49.836 align:middle
And the fact that Congress is
debating a fairly large bill

55:50.226 --> 55:52.296 align:middle
today suggests that,
that-you know,

55:52.296 --> 55:54.696 align:middle
suggests that something
fairly substantial is going

55:54.696 --> 55:58.006 align:middle
to get done, we hope-that
what's being discussed is,

55:58.006 --> 56:01.006 align:middle
you know, is, is of some size.

56:01.726 --> 56:05.066 align:middle
In terms of what is a
sustainable level, I think,

56:05.066 --> 56:09.006 align:middle
you know, it's-the question
is-we've always looked at debt

56:09.006 --> 56:11.396 align:middle
to GDP, and we're very
high by that measure.

56:11.396 --> 56:14.076 align:middle
By some other measures,
we're actually not that high.

56:14.076 --> 56:18.036 align:middle
In particular, you can look at
the real interest rate payments,

56:18.036 --> 56:19.906 align:middle
the amount of, what
does it cost?

56:20.476 --> 56:22.616 align:middle
And from that standpoint,
if you-if you sort

56:22.616 --> 56:26.666 align:middle
of take real interest costs of
the federal deficit and divide

56:26.666 --> 56:30.196 align:middle
that by GDP, we're actually-you
know, we're actually

56:30.196 --> 56:32.016 align:middle
on a more sustainable
fiscal path if you look

56:32.016 --> 56:32.726 align:middle
at it through those eyes.

56:32.726 --> 56:35.156 align:middle
Again, these are-these
are issues for Congress.

56:35.986 --> 56:39.006 align:middle
But, you know, I'll just
say in the near term,

56:39.006 --> 56:43.176 align:middle
I think fiscal-the case for,
for fiscal is, is strong.

56:43.536 --> 56:45.646 align:middle
And I'm certainly hoping-I
think it will be very good

56:45.646 --> 56:47.826 align:middle
for the economy if, if we
did get something soon.

56:47.826 --> 56:49.356 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

56:49.546 --> 56:52.626 align:middle
Well, the argument for
continuing to spend is

56:52.626 --> 56:54.576 align:middle
that low interest rates
make it affordable.

56:54.576 --> 56:56.556 align:middle
Do you worry you
get in a situation

56:56.556 --> 56:58.286 align:middle
where you would have
congressional interference

56:58.286 --> 56:59.676 align:middle
in monetary policymaking

56:59.676 --> 57:01.736 align:middle
or at least feel
significant political pressure

57:02.006 --> 57:05.846 align:middle
to keep rates low because
the country can't afford

57:05.846 --> 57:07.666 align:middle
to pay a significant
interest bill?

57:09.456 --> 57:09.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

57:09.836 --> 57:11.986 align:middle
Yeah, I mean, that's-I
think we're a very,

57:11.986 --> 57:13.406 align:middle
very long way from that.

57:13.696 --> 57:17.686 align:middle
You know, the, the Congress
has given the Fed independence

57:17.836 --> 57:19.646 align:middle
on the condition that we
stick to our knitting.

57:20.026 --> 57:22.826 align:middle
We try very hard to do that.

57:22.826 --> 57:26.206 align:middle
And I, I think that's,

57:26.206 --> 57:28.146 align:middle
that's what people
call fiscal dominance.

57:28.146 --> 57:31.026 align:middle
And I, I think we're just a
very, very long way from that.

57:31.026 --> 57:35.896 align:middle
I think, you know, if
we-if we do our jobs well

57:35.896 --> 57:38.996 align:middle
and support the economy and
achieve maximum employment

57:38.996 --> 57:41.156 align:middle
and stable prices, keep the
financial system stable,

57:41.156 --> 57:43.286 align:middle
I don't think that
that is something

57:43.286 --> 57:44.516 align:middle
that will-that I
would worry about,

57:44.516 --> 57:45.536 align:middle
certainly not in the near term.

57:45.776 --> 57:46.156 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

57:47.476 --> 57:48.486 align:middle
Thank you very much.

57:48.486 --> 57:48.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

57:48.746 --> 57:49.026 align:middle
Thank you.

57:49.026 --> 57:49.696 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

57:49.696 --> 57:53.496 align:middle
Good afternoon.

